\par\par \b \uc2  HK Dlr Weaker Early; Hit By Some Switching Into US$ Assets \b0\par\par\uc2 382 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 April 1999\par 05:49\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG (Dow Jones)--The Hong Kong dollar is weaker early Thursday, hit by some switching into higher-yielding U.S. dollar assets, and may weaken further to HK$7.7500 per U.S. dollar during the session if the switching continues. \par \par \uc2  It's also being eroded by concerns sparked by a report that the authorities may take over a small Hong Kong bank. \par \par \uc2  Around 0340 GMT (11:40 p.m. EDT Wednesday), the Hong Kong dollar was quoted at HK$7.7485 against the U.S. dollar, slightly weaker than the HK$7.7483 quoted late Wednesday. The local unit has traded between HK$7.7480 and HK$7.7492 so far Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "Hong Kong dollar interest rates are too low," said a local bank treasurer, explaining the weakness in the local unit. \par \par \uc2  The volatile overnight Hong Kong Interbank Offered Rate is quoted at 4.125%, up from 4.00% late Wednesday, but still well below the 4.75% level of its U.S. counterpart. \par \par \uc2  The treasurer said switching into U.S. dollars could push the Hong Kong dollar to HK$7.7500 per U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  That's still comfortably far from the HK$7.7522 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  level at which the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  guarantees to support the Hong Kong dollar. \par \par \uc2  Sentiment is also being hurt by a local newspaper report that the HKMA was considering taking over a small Hong Kong bank. But the HKMA and management of the bank both said later there were no plans to do so. \par \par \uc2  Forward \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  premiums, a good bellwether to confidence in the local unit, are generally lower, suggesting that any weakness in the Hong Kong dollar in the current session is likely to be short-lived and offset by mutual funds buying into the stock market rally. \par \par \uc2  In the forward \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  the one-month premium is quoted at five points, up from zero late Wednesday. The three-month premium is quoted at 33, down from 45. The six-month premium is quoted at 180, down from 185. The one-year is at 790, down from 870. \par \par \uc2  -By Sean Kennedy; (852) 2832 2336; sean.kennedy@cor.dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 22-04-99 \par \par \uc2  0349GMT \par \par \uc2  1053 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv4m030p7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
