\par\par \b \uc2  HK Dollar Touch Stronger Late; Market Watches Land Sale \b0\par\par\uc2 357 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 April 1999\par 09:29\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG (Dow Jones)--The Hong Kong dollar is a touch stronger in thin trade late Tuesday as most institutions sit on the sidelines while the government tests corporate confidence with the first government land sale in more than a year. \par \par \uc2  Around 0640 GMT (2:40 a.m. EDT), the Hong Kong dollar is quoted at HK$7.7483 against the U.S. dollar, barely changed from HK$7.7484 late Monday. The U.S. dollar has traded between HK$7.7480 and HK$7.7485 so far Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there's little interest in the spot Hong Kong dollar market, and most banks are more interested in how the government land sale goes. The sale started at 0615 GMT Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "All eyes are on the land auction," said a local bank treasurer. The land sale is seen as a bellwether of corporate confidence in the property sector, which at one point last year was down by around 60% from its 1997 highs due to the Asian crisis. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said they expect a reasonably strong result from the land sale. \par \par \uc2  They also downplayed a fall in the Hang Seng Index, a major focus for the spot Hong Kong dollar market in recent sessions. While the Hang Seng Index is down 0.8% in mid-afternoon trade, dealers said it's just profit-taking after a total rise of almost 7% over the previous two sessions. \par \par \uc2  They expect the spot Hong Kong dollar to hug its current trading range, which is near the so-called \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  level of HK$7.7520, helped by expected further inflows of cash from institutional investors. \par \par \uc2  In the forward \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, the one-month premium is unchanged from late Monday. The three-month premium is quoted at 57 points, up from 49 points. The six-month premium is quoted at 200 points, up from 180. The one-year premium is quoted at 890 points, up from 850. \par \par \uc2  -By Sean Kennedy; (852) 2832 2336;sean.kennedy@cor.dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 20-04-99 \par \par \uc2  0729GMT \par \par \uc2  1053 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv4k033bn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
