\par\par \b \uc2  HK Dollar Stronger Early; Backed By Cash Inflow Hopes \b0\par\par\uc2 275 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 April 1999\par 04:38\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG (Dow Jones)--The Hong Kong dollar is sharply stronger against the U.S. dollar early Monday, buoyed by expectations of further inflows of hot money into the blue-chip Hang Seng Index. \par \par \uc2  Around 0200 GMT (10:00 p.m. Sunday EDT), the Hong Kong dollar is quoted at HK$7.7484 against the U.S. dollar, from HK$7.7496 late Friday. That's considerably stronger than the HK$7.7519 level seen as the level at which the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Money is still coming in... it's hot money targeting the stock market. The market's pretty bullish," a dealer at an Australian bank said, noting that the spot Hong Kong dollar hit HK$7.7472 to the U.S. dollar in early trading Friday, because of a 4.4% rally in the Hang Seng Index. \par \par \uc2  "There is some European money coming in," a dealer at a local bank said. \par \par \uc2  The cash appears to be fueling gains on the Hang Seng index, which was up 320.31 points, or 2.6%, to 12,810.61, though it's since edged back. \par \par \uc2  In the forward \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, the one-month premium is quoted at five points, up from three late Friday. The three-month premium is quoted at 55 points, unchanged from Friday. The six-month premium is quoted at 210 points, down from 220 previously. The one-year premium is steady, at 910 points. \par \par \uc2  - By Sean Kennedy; (852) 2832 2336; sean.kennedy@cor.dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 19-04-99 \par \par \uc2  0238GMT \par \par \uc2  1053 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv4j02