\par\par \b \uc2  Hong Kong Bankers Association Trims Rates By A Quarter-Point \b0\par\par\uc2 951 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 April 1999\par 12:10\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG -(Dow Jones)- The Hong Kong Association of Banks said Friday it lowered deposit rates by one-quarter percentage point to 3.75%. The cut marked the first adjustment to the benchmark short-term rates since Jan. 8, when the association reduced them by a quarter point to 4%. \par \par \uc2  Major banks - including Bank of China, Standard Chartered Bank and HSBC Holdings PLC's local units, Hongkong Bank and Hang Seng Bank - followed suit, trimming their prime rates to 8.5% from 8.75%, effective Monday. Changes in the association's deposit rate, a benchmark in Hong Kong, typically are followed by matching changes in individual banks' prime lending rate. \par \par \uc2  The banks association, which meets every Friday to discuss possible changes to the deposit rates, controls rates on savings deposits of less than one-week maturity. \par \par \uc2  Liu Jinbao, association chairman, said he sees room for further interest-rate reductions if both domestic and external economic conditions remain stable. Liu , who represents Bank of China's Hong Kong and Macau branch, on the Hong Kong rate-setting cartel said he expects Friday's interest-rate cut to "help the overall economy in Hong Kong" and ease pressure on banks in the city, which are engaged in a battle for mortgage business. \par \par \uc2  Asked if the unilateral rate cut - made by association without a corresponding move in the U.S. by the Federal Reserve Board - would fuel speculation in rallying Hong Kong stocks, Liu said the cartel had considered all factors in making its decision. Hong Kong tends to follow adjustments in the U.S. benchmark federal-funds rate because of the Hong Kong dollar's peg to the U.S. dollar at a rate of HK$7.80. \par \par \uc2  Liu added that the banks association supports the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 's proposal to begin deregulating the local interest-rate regime. A report commissioned last year by the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Hong Kong's de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , recommended a gradual deregulation starting after 1999. Last month, Liu said the association submitted its own proposal to the authority, but provided no details. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and money markets shrugged off the lower rates, but some traders said the territory's stock market could see some profit-taking when trading resumes Monday. \par \par \uc2  Traders said they had been expecting the cut all week, so the spot Hong Kong dollar, money market rates and forward \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  premiums already reflected the decision by the banks association to reduce the rate on savings deposits of under one week to 3.75%. "It's pretty much expected. I think 25 basis points (one-quarter percentage point) was pretty much what the market was hoping for," said Mike Powell, head of interest rates for HSBC Markets. \par \par \uc2  Bankers noted that short-term money market rates have fallen steadily in the past few days, reflecting the market's confidence of another rate cut. The volatile overnight rate, which hit 5.75% last week, was quoted at 4.75% late Friday, even with the comparable U.S. benchmark rate. \par \par \uc2  Powell described the cut as just "official rubber-stamping of what the market has done to short-dated rates." \par \par \uc2  With past surprise rate cuts, the Hong Kong dollar has weakened immediately after the banks association decision, but traders noted that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was actually slightly higher after Friday's cut. The Hong Kong dollar was trading at HK$7.7480 immediately after the news, slightly stronger than HK$7.7491 earlier in the day and much stronger than the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Friday \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate of HK$7.7509. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar is expected to remain stable between HK$7.7480 and HK$7.7500 against the U.S. dollar, Commonwealth Bank of Australia treasurer Andrew Fung said. \par \par \uc2  Traders downplayed notions that lower interest rates would lure back speculators, who last attacked the Hong Kong dollar in August 1998, sparking \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  to spend HK$118.1 billion in stock and futures markets squeezing the speculators out. \par \par \uc2  Stock-market investors had been betting on a rate cut all week, helping lift the Hang Seng Index to its highest level in more than a year. Friday, the index jumped 186.26 points, or 1.6%, to close at 11914.10. The market is likely to dip Monday, said Michael Ng, dealing director at Sassoon Securities Ltd. "As usual, investors buy on rumors, sell on news, so the rate cut is already discounted in the market," he said. \par \par \uc2  Ng noted the April Hang Seng index futures contract closed at about an 89-point discount to the cash market, indicating investors are betting the market's 6.7% rise this week may be running out of steam. \par \par \uc2  But others aren't as willing to commit to a decline Monday in the Hang Seng index because the rally also was fueled by a huge inflow of overseas funds via large investment banks and brokers. Adrian Ngan of BNP Prime Peregrine Securities (HK) Ltd. said he's not convinced the inflow of funds from the U.S. is about to abate. \par \par \uc2  "U.S. institutions continue to pump money into Asian markets and that will continue," said Adrian Ngan, head of research at BNP Prime Peregrine Securities (HK) Ltd. Overseas investors expected a rate cut, he said. Ngan also said property stocks will gain from the rate-cut news, particularly ahead of the government's resumption of its land sales April 20 after a nine-month hiatus. \par \par \uc2  -By Sean Kennedy and Kirsti Hastings; (852)2802-7002; sean.kennedy@cor.dowjones.com, kirsti.hastings@cor.dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1999 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020329dv4902909\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
