\par\par \b \uc2  HK Dlr Dn Slightly Late,Weathers Start Of Phased Devaluation \b0\par\par\uc2 459 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 April 1999\par 09:50\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG (Dow Jones)--The Hong Kong dollar is a touch weaker late Thursday in quiet pre-holiday trade as players assess the impact of the first day of "phased devaluation" by the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Around 0645 GMT (1:45 a.m. EST), the Hong Kong dollar was quoted at HK$7.7500 against the U.S. dollar, up from HK$7.7499 late Wednesday. It has traded between HK$7.7498 and HK$7.7501 during the session, with traders reporting several small trades done at HK$7.7501. \par \par \uc2  The market is now interpreting that level as the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 's new \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  level. \par \par \uc2  On Thursday, the convertibility rate moved to HK$7.7501 to the U.S. dollar and market participants were seen testing that level to see whether the HKMA would step in to bolster the local unit. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said activity was subdued, with many players sidelined ahead of a three-day market holiday. \par \par \uc2  They said the spot Hong Kong dollar is well-supported at its current level, despite the start of phased devaluation. Under the plan, the HKMA will move the convertibility rate one pip (0.0001 point) a day until the spot Hong Kong dollar reverts to HK$7.80, the level at which it was originally pegged to the U.S. dollar in 1983. The level moved to HK$7.7501 Thursday, from HK$7.7500 previously. \par \par \uc2  The convertibility rate is that at which the HKMA guarantees banks can convert U.S. dollars into the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the Hong Kong dollar is supported by firm short-term interest rates despite the phased depreciation. A local bank treasurer said he suspects one large local bank, acting at the behest of the HKMA, lent money aggressively early in the session to bring the overnight rate down after it spiked due to a fall in liquidity. \par \par \uc2  The volatile overnight Hong Kong Interbank Offered Rate is quoted at 5.25%, down from 5.75% late Wednesday, and from 6.00% early in the current session. \par \par \uc2  Another local dealer said he doesn't expect the overnight rate to fall further, but to retain a premium of at least 75 basis points over the U.S. overnight rate. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the forward \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, the one-month premium is\par quoted at 55 points, up from 48 late Wednesday. The three-month premium\par is at 150 points, unchanged. The six-month rate is at 450 points, also\par unchanged. The one-year rate is at 1,450 points, up from 1,430\par previously.\par    -Sean Kennedy; (852) 2832 2336;\par     sean.kennedy@cor.dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (Corrected 0841GMT)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires 01-04-99\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 0750GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1053 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv4102j91\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
