\par\par \b \uc2  HKD Hits HK$7.75 -2:Still Questions About  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Level \b0\par\par\uc2 183 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 April 1999\par 03:42\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 HONG KONG\b0 \highlight\uc2  (Dow Jones)--The \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar hit HK$7.75 per U.S. dollar in early trading Thursday, and traders say it may become slightly weaker shortly in a test of whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at HK$7.7501. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar's weakening was expected, due to the HKMA's one-pip (0.0001-point) movement of the rate at which it guarantees banks can convert U.S. dollars into local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The conversion level of HK$7.75 was set last September. At that time the HKMA said it would move it on a daily basis over 500 days from April 1 until it reached the official peg rate of HK$7.80 per U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  The conversion rate moved to HK$7.7501 Thursday morning, but dealers are still uncertain - because the HKMA hasn't explicitly said - whether the quasi-\b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate is moving in synch with the conversion rate. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 01-04-99 \par \par \uc2  0142GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv4102imx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
