\par\par \b \uc2  HK Bank Association Says Not Yet Time To Cut Rates \b0\par\par\uc2 270 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1999\par 12:24\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG (Dow Jones)--The Hong Kong Association of Banks decided to hold interest rates steady at its weekly meeting Friday, citing the recent weakness of the territory's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  HKAB left the deposit rate unchanged at 4.00% at its meeting, despite week-long speculation that the rate-setting body planned a 25-basis point rate cut. \par \par \uc2  HKAB Chairman Liu Jinbao said the Association is "very cautious" about the Hong Kong dollar, which has been weak in recent trading sessions. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong dollar has been flirting with HK$7.7500, seen as a trigger point for defensive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the territory's de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It's been under pressure because already low local short-term interbank rates have prompted outflows in favor of higher-yielding U.S. money market instruments. \par \par \uc2  But Liu said there's still room for a rate cut in the future because Hong Kong's real interest rates are still high. \par \par \uc2  Liu said HKAB also opted for prudence at its latest weekly meeting because it's uncertain where U.S. rates are going. "Hong Kong cannot rely on an interest rate cut to solve its economic problems," he added. \par \par \uc2  He also said he sees no need for China to cut its interest rates because it has stable economic growth. \par \par \uc2  Any change in the deposit rate, a key lending rate in Hong Kong, is normally followed by matching changes in individual banks' prime lending rates which now stand at 8.75%. \par \par