\par\par \b \uc2  Source Says Hong Kong To Sell Shares Bought For Market Support \b0\par\par\uc2 219 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1999\par 18:58\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG -(Dow Jones)- The \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Hong Kong has decided to sell off a large portion of its holdings in local companies, whose shares it bought last summer to support the Hong Kong stock market, The Nihon Keizai Shimbun reported in its Saturday morning edition, citing an unnamed senior financial official, according to Japan's Nikkei news service. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong's authority hasn't yet set a deadline for the sell-off, which will involve just over HK100 billion worth of shares. The unnamed senior financial official said, "We will be extremely careful not to upset the market." \par \par \uc2  Last summer, the authority \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the stock market to deter speculative selling of \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollars and shares of local companies. \par \par \uc2  Most of the shares are held in the territory's foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserve fund. The sale would lower the fund's proportion of Hong Kong stocks to 5% of its total assets - down from the current 17%, or about HK150 billion, of the fund's total assets of around HK910 billion. \par \par \uc2  To ease the impact on the market, the authority will consider such measures as convertible bond issuance and stock buybacks, the senior official said. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1999 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020329dv35020ve\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
