\par\par \b \uc2  HK Dlr/Late -2: Renewed Rumors Of Yuan Devaluation \b0\par\par\uc2 178 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Januar 1999\par 10:10\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Renewed rumors of a devaluation in China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yuan, were also spooking the market, said a trader with a local bank. \par \par \uc2  On top of that, news that Argentina will seek a monetary treaty with the U.S. in a step toward dollarizing its economy weighed heavily on Hong Kong dollar forwards. \par \par \uc2  But the nervousness was hardly reflected in the spot market, where the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued to move in a tight range. \par \par \uc2  The trader with a local bank said players wanted to keep the spot rate steady so that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  wouldn't have to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market. The de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is believed to be ready to buy the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  if it touches the HK$7.75 level. \par \par \uc2  Looking ahead to next week, traders warned that there could be more volatility ahead as the crisis in Brazil continues to unfold. \par \par \uc2  -By Patty Zhao; (852)2802-7002; patty.zhao@cor.dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 22-01-99 \par \par \uc2  0910GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv1m00kyj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
