\par\par \b \uc2  HK dollar bumped but not budged by Brazil jitters. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Andrea Ricci \par \uc2 750 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Januar 1999\par 08:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, Jan 22 (Reuters) - The Hong Kong dollar came under modest pressure on Friday amid continued worries over Brazil, but traders and analysts said there was little sign of speculative activity against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg. \par \par \uc2  "I don't see any signs of a speculative attack against the Hong Kong dollar today, certainly not comparable to that of last August," said Stephen Cheung, a director of Jardine Fleming Bank. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong dollar was at HK$7.7480/90 per U.S. dollar at 0720 GMT on Friday, slightly weaker than 7.7468/80 at Thursday's close. Forward rates were up modestly, more so in the longer end than the short end, while interest rates were slightly higher. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg came under intense pressure in August 1998 in what the government described as a "double play" game by hedge funds to push up interest rates by attacking the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and then take profits on short stock positions after the share market reacted badly to higher rates. \par \par \uc2  The government aggressively bought stocks in controversial \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to foil speculators that month. In September, it unveiled a seven-point plan to shore up the 15-year old \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system which included liquidity enhancing measures and promises of greater transparency. \par \par \uc2  The measures have contributed greatly to maintaining the Hong Kong dollar's stability even as Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  troubles spark market jitters worldwide, traders and analysts said. \par \par \uc2  In the past, balances in the Hong Kong banking system could be shifted by the slightest outflow of U.S. dollars, but now a huge flow is required to cause an interest rate surge, said Craig Chan, regional treasury economist at Standard Chartered Bank. \par \par \uc2  Hedge funds' access to funding also has diminished in recent months, especially after the well-publicised failure of U.S. fund Long-Term Capital Management late last year. Analysts noted also that speculators don't have much to gain in Hong Kong if they, as the government has insisted, were intent last year not on breaking the peg but in busting stock prices. \par \par \uc2  With government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the share market still a threat, speculators might think twice before selling stocks short and assaulting the local dollar. \par \par \uc2  "Of course, one argument against the idea of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is that \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  already owns a big chunk of the stock market and they've already said the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last year was extraordinary," said Lehman Brothers economist Rob Subbaraman. "Do they really want to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again?" \par \par \uc2  Money market traders said activity has been dominated by position-squaring and concentrated in the long end rather than the short end of the curve. \par \par \uc2  Chordio Chan, head of money markets at Citibank, noted that the overnight rate has been trading below the U.S. discount rate for some time, although there was a short-lived spike on Thursday on rumours the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  was draining funds. \par \par \uc2  "But the short end still remains on the soft side, in contrast to what we saw in 1997 and 1998, when everytime there was an attack on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , short rates tended to surge and rise higher than the longer end, rendering a flatter curve," Chan said. "But this time, we have seen the curve flattening." \par \par \uc2  A Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  representative in New York said on Thursday the peg would stand and that Brazil's devaluation would have no impact on Hong Kong's economy. \par \par \uc2  Still, Brazil's difficulties are sure to keep markets in Hong Kong on edge, especially if its problems are seen spreading. \par \par \uc2  "Brazil could cause renewed pressure on the Hong Kong dollar for two reasons - the possibility of a global growth slowdown and a credit shock that causes risk premiums to rise," said Kate O'Donoghue, regional economist at Barclays Capital. \par \par \uc2  Worries about the stability of China's yuan in the face of slower world growth would also unnerve Hong Kong markets, despite Beijing's repeated vows to maintain its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  On Thursday, Argentina, which like Hong Kong has a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system, floated the idea of adopting the U.S. dollar, a move the government said was unrelated to Brazil's woes. \par \par \uc2  While dollarisation by Argentina would have little direct effect on Hong Kong, it would certainly raise anew questions about the long-term viability of a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  ((Hong Kong Newsroom (852) 2843-6371; Fax (852) 2845-0636; hongkong.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv1m063wk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
