\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore rivals Hong Kong as Asia's choice. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Valerie Lee \par \uc2 914 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Januar 1999\par 17:40\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE (Reuters) - It used to be a no-contest: Hong Kong was easily the place of choice to do business in Asia. \par \par \uc2  Singapore, swaddled by red tape and policed by a stern \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was a distant runner-up, a destination of necessity. \par \par \uc2  But more than a year into the Asian crisis, Singapore appears to have edged in front and is winning kudos from investors, credit-rating agencies, securities analysts and foreign media for having one of Asia's best-run economies and financial sectors. \par \par \uc2  It has at last thrown down a serious challenge to Hong Kong. \par \par \uc2  "Singapore has finally got it right," one managing director of a regional securities firm in Singapore said. \par \par \uc2  "It is not about doctrine or ideology, it is all finally driven by commercial considerations - what can we do to get your business to thrive? \par \par \uc2  "That is the best way to win in this game with Hong Kong," he said. \par \par \uc2  Some of the world's biggest companies are putting their faith in Singapore, including Caltex Petroleum Corp., which said in October it would relocate its headquarters from Dallas. It is the first multinational to establish its global head office here. \par \par \uc2  The fight for supremacy between Singapore and Hong Kong began last year in the securities industry. \par \par \uc2  Trading of Hang Seng index futures on the Singapore International Monetary Exchange marked the official start of hostilities, said I.D.E.A. senior economist Jacqueline Ong. \par \par \uc2  And the battle is intense, with newspapers joining the fray. \par \par \uc2  "Hong Kong and Singapore seem to have swapped places in the eyes of the international media," the Singapore Business Times said recently. \par \par \uc2  "Hong Kong, which used to be the media darling up until the handover from Britain to China, is getting uncharacteristic bad press. Singapore, on the other hand, has been getting favourable reviews." \par \par \uc2  The issue was costs, Ong said, and a Singapore government S$10.5 billion ($6.36 billion) package of cost-cutting measures has boosted the city state's competitiveness. \par \par \uc2  The centrepiece of the November package was a 50 percent cut in employer contributions to a state pension fund. Trade unions accepted the move without any apparent rancour. \par \par \uc2  "While both cities provide reasonably good infrastructure, it boils down to cost competitiveness," Ong said. \par \par \uc2  On this front, Hong Kong's policy of pegging the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the U.S. dollar had become a handicap, she said. \par \par \uc2  I.D.E.A., a research house, made a comparative study of cost competitiveness of the two cities, and Hong Kong came up short. \par \par \uc2  Among other things, the study looked at wages, rent, interest costs, and utilities and service costs. \par \par \uc2  "Our study shows that overall cost in Singapore started to correct downwards as early as Q1 1998 ... however, costs in Hong Kong continued in an upward trajectory," Ong said in the report. \par \par \uc2  Assuming its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg remained, Hong Kong property prices and wages needed to fall further this year to compete, she added. \par \par \uc2  The average manager in Hong Kong earns double the salary of a Singapore manager, according to recent data from U.S. funds research company William Mercer. The average in Hong Kong was $62.80 an hour and in Singapore $30.70 an hour. \par \par \uc2  William Mercer said rentals for a small, two-bedroom apartment in Hong Kong averaged $6,400 a month in a "good area," compared with $2,002 for a comparable apartment in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  Singapore has also stolen a march on Hong Kong in reforming its financial industry in the midst of the Asian crisis. \par \par \uc2  Last month, Washington-based Heritage Foundation said Hong Kong could lose its position as the freest economy in the world to its arch rival Singapore. \par \par \uc2  In 1998, the Singapore government placed billions of dollars with private fund managers to boost the industry and issued its first 10-year bond to deepen the official debt market. \par \par \uc2  It made it easier for foreign entities to list Singapore-dollar shares and issue Singapore-dollar bonds, raised bank disclosure standards and encouraged bank mergers. \par \par \uc2  As investors lauded Singapore's move to a more open market, \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in their stock market and Malaysia shut its doors on investors with capital controls. \par \par \uc2  Singapore's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore (MAS), mounted a global marketing campaign, promoting itself to investors in the United States, Europe and Asia. \par \par \uc2  It boasted that in 1998, 15 more foreign firms were given the status of approved overseas headquarters and finance and treasury centres in Singapore, which carries with it tax benefits. \par \par \uc2  They included Asea Brown Boveri, Ericsson, Hitachi, Hoechst and NCR Asia. \par \par \uc2  The MAS said several asset management companies - including Morgan Grenfell (Asia) Ltd., ABN-Amro Asset Management and Morgan Stanley Dean Witter Investment Management Co. - were either consolidating their management of Asian funds in Singapore or had selected it as their hub for regional asset management. \par \par \uc2  Russell Whitehouse, regional finance director of Guardian Insurance, which recently moved its regional base from Hong Kong to Singapore, said Singapore's location was also attractive. \par \par \uc2  "This was part of the region our growth was coming from," he told Reuters. He also cited the city's efficient airport and its reputation as a safe and good place to live. \par \par \uc2  Singapore might now have its nose in front of Hong Kong in many respects, but the contest is never over, analysts said. Some cautioned that Hong Kong was still hard on Singapore's heels. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv17077vz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
