\par\par \b \uc2  Morgan Stanley Chairman Says U.S. Economy Will Grow 3.6% in 1998 \b0\par\par\uc2 528 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 November 1998\par 14:17\par \uc2  Dow Jones Online News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG -(Dow Jones)- The U.S. economy will grow at a steady 3.6% rate this year and the Federal Reserve Board is likely to lower interest rates again by the year end, the chairman and chief executive officer of Morgan Stanley Dean Witter & Co. said Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "Our growth forecast for this year remains basically unchanged at 3.6%," despite the "global economic contagion" in the wake of the emerging market crises in Asia, Latin America and Russia, Philip Purcell said at a business luncheon in Hong Kong. \par \par \uc2  Purcell predicted an economic slowdown in the U.S. next year. However, "despite the global economic woes, we're still looking for 2.3% (gross domestic product) growth in the U.S. in 1999," he added. \par \par \uc2  He said the Fed is breaking from the anti-inflationary policy it has held in the past 19 years as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  tries to respond directly to troubles in emerging markets. \par \par \uc2  "The main danger is still a worldwide credit crunch, and I am hopeful there will be further easing of monetary policy by year end," he said. \par \par \uc2  Analysts remain divided on the prospect of a rate cut ahead of the Federal Open Market Committee meeting later Tuesday. The rate-setting body will meet again in December, its last meeting this year. \par \par \uc2  Purcell said there are signs that the worst of the Asian crisis might be over. \par \par \uc2  "As far as Asia is concerned, while it's a bit early to bring on the bulls, we do think that there are a number of reasons not to be bearish," he said. \par \par \uc2  He said that lower interest rates in the U.S., coupled with Japan's willingness to help and sustained growth in China, will benefit other Asian countries and help keep regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  steady. \par \par \uc2  "We believe we've seen the worst of the recession in (Asia) in 1998," Purcell said, predicting that investors will start to return to the region next year. \par \par \uc2  He said Japan and China will be "the twin pillars on which Southeast Asia's recovery will rest." Japan needs to stick to its banking reforms, while "China, in our view, will maintain the current value of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he noted. \par \par \uc2  Purcell said he is confident that Hong Kong will retain its status as a world-class financial center. While he didn't directly comment on the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  government's massive market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he praised Financial Secretary Donald Tsang's efforts to assure international investors that the territory is committed to the free market principle. \par \par \uc2  "Foreign investors were shaken by the moves (taken by the Hong Kong government). Some of them were punished financially...(but) confidence is returning that (Hong Kong) is going to be a free market," Purcell said. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong government spent HK$118 billion acquiring blue-chip stocks in the last two weeks of August, in a bid to fight off international speculators and maintain stability in local equity and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  -By Patty Zhao; 852 2802 7002; pzhao@ap.org \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1998 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020329dubh01gec\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
