\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS - HK joins land, exchange fund assets. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Andrea Ricci \par \uc2 999 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 November 1998\par 14:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, Nov 17 (Reuters) - The Hong Kong government on Tuesday said it would merge the assets of its Land Fund with its Exchange Fund in a bid to align investment policy, reduce costs and boost returns to its fiscal reserves. \par \par \uc2  "With the alignment, the Land Fund will be able to enjoy the same returns achieved by the Exchange Fund, thereby allowing the Land Fund to enjoy a more steady return at reduced volatility," Treasury Secretary Denise Yue told reporters. \par \par \uc2  "There will also be enlarged fiscal reserves," she said. \par \par \uc2  Before the merger, the assets of the Land Fund, which holds the proceeds from government land sales, and the Exchange Fund, which provides backing for the Hong Kong dollar's peg to the U.S. dollar, were managed separately. \par \par \uc2  The Land Fund, which was 40.7 percent invested in cash at the end of September, typically earns lower returns than the more aggressive Exchange Fund while suffering more volatility. \par \par \uc2  The move, which was effective as of October 31, 1998, fulfilled the objective announced in February this year by Financial Secretary Donald Tsang of aligning the investment policy of the two funds by next April, Yue said. \par \par \uc2  Land Fund assets, which were about HK$211.4 billion as of October 31, would be placed with the Exchange Fund for the account of the Land Fund, the government said. \par \par \uc2  The fiscal reserves already placed with the Exchange Fund amounted to HK$203.1 billion as of October 31, it said. \par \par \uc2  "This is a pure and simple way to handle the fiscal reserves, including the Land Fund," said Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  (HKMA) chief Joseph Yam. \par \par \uc2  With the addition of Land Fund assets, the Exchange Fund would total HK$946 billion, he said. \par \par \uc2  Yam said adding HK$211 billion to the Exchange Fund might "psychologically boost" sentiment toward the Hong Kong dollar peg, but he added that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in circulation already was covered many times over by the Exchange Fund. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is linked to the U.S. dollar through a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system, which maintains the pegged rate of HK$7.8 per US$1 through automatic adjustments in liquidity. \par \par \uc2  After the merger, the Hong Kong equity portfolio previously held in the Land Fund would go to the Exchange Fund Investment Ltd (EFIL) for management in the same manner as the Hong Kong shares acquired by the Exchange Fund through the government's massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the local stock market in August. \par \par \uc2  EFIL was set up to manage the HK$118 billion in blue chip shares the government bought in August to fend off speculators. \par \par \uc2  All Hong Kong equity holdings of the Hong Kong government would, therefore, be consolidated for management by EFIL. \par \par \uc2  At HK$12 billion at the end of September, the size of the Land Fund's Hong Kong equity holdings is relatively small compared with the Exchange Fund stock portfolio. \par \par \uc2  The government has said that it has not sold any shares acquired for the Exchange Fund during the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but Yam said the Land Fund may have bought or sold shares in the past couple of months as part of its normal investment \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  EFIL would be asked to consider disclosing the details of the enlarged Hong Kong equity portfolio as a result of the merger, the government said. \par \par \uc2  The merging of Land Fund and Exchange Fund assets was not designed to facilitate equity investment by the government, but rather to streamline investment policy for the fiscal reserves, Treasury Secretary Yue said. \par \par \uc2  The long-term strategy on the government holdings of Hong Kong equity would be examined by the HKMA in the context of the overall review on asset allocation in the management of the Exchange Fund, the government said. \par \par \uc2  After the merging of its assets with the Exchange Fund, the Land Fund still would remain a separate government fund and the use of its holdings for government spending would remain subject to approval by Hong Kong's legislative council, it added. \par \par \uc2  - Hong Kong newsroom (852) 2843 6371; fax (852) 2845 0636 \par \par \uc2  - Email: hongkong.newsroom@reuters.com. \par \par \uc2  It said the move would allow the Land Fund to enjoy the same rate of return as the Exchange Fund. \par \par \uc2  Land Fund assets were about HK$211.4 billion as of October 31. This would be placed with the Exchange Fund for the account of the Land Fund. \par \par \uc2  The fiscal reserves already placed with the Exchange Fund amounted to HK$203.1 billion as of October 31, 1998, it said. The merger was an investment decision taken by Financial Secretary Donald Tsang. \par \par \uc2  After the merger, the Hong Kong equity portfolio previously held in the Land Fund would go to the Exchange Fund Investment Ltd (EFIL) for management in the same manner as the Hong Kong equity portfolio acquired by the Exchange Fund in the market \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  in August. \par \par \uc2  EFIL was set up to manage HK$118 billion in blue chip shares which the government bought during an August \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to fend off speculators. \par \par \uc2  All \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  equity holdings of the Hong Kong government would, therefore, be consolidated for management by EFIL. It said although this reduced flexibility in handling such holdings, it was in line with the high degree of transparency expected of the Exchange Fund. \par \par \uc2  The size of the Land Fund's Hong Kong equity holdings is relatively small compared with the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  equity portfolio purchased during the August \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But, the statement said, EFIL would be asked to consider disclosing the details of the enlarged Hong Kong equity portfolio as a result of the merger. \par \par \uc2  The long-term strategy on the government holding of Hong Kong equity would be examined by the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the context of the overall review on asset allocation in the management of the Exchange Fund, the statement said. - Hong Kong Newsroom (852) 2843 6441; fax (852) 2845 0636 - Email: hongkong.newsroom@reuters.com. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010926dubh04brl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
