\par\par \b \uc2  HK's Last Governor -3: Advocates HK Property Sector Changes \b0\par\par\uc2 269 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Oktober 1998\par 06:21\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Patten also hit out at Hong Kong's big property developers, calling for changes to the sector, traditionally one of the key drivers in Hong Kong's economy but which has seen property prices slump as much as 50% from last year's peak. \par \par \uc2  Patten said that more free-market principles are needed to be brought into play in the sector, which has been dominated by a a select number of companies and overseen by a developer-friendly government. \par \par \uc2  "The right approach in the long-term is more reliance on the market and less reliance on what has been portrayed as a combination of cartelization and municipalization," said Patten. \par \par \uc2  Patten downplayed the role of government planning, saying that civil servants, as capable as Hong Kong's are, can't outsmart the market. \par \par \uc2  Patten also defended the August market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which spent HK$118.1 billion to buy up blue-chip stocks and futures to ward off speculators, leaving the government a substantial shareholder in some of \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 's largest companies. \par \par \uc2  "I don't believe (the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) means that \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  has abandoned its traditional faith in markets and market solutions...It's the free-market economics, not government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , that has transformed \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 " from a low value-added to high value-added economy, he said. \par \par \uc2  Patten also dismissed views that China is under growing pressure to devalue its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yuan, adding that a devaluation wouldn't help boost China's trade performance. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 30-10-98 \par \par \uc2  0521GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010917duau00mn1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
