\par\par \b \uc2  HK Govt Foreign Reserves Could Fall To $20B - Analyst \b0\par\par\uc2 289 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 September 1998\par 06:34\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG (Dow Jones)--The Hong Kong government may have spent as much as $3 billion dollars in the local equity and futures markets in September, according a report published by Prudential-Bache Securities (\b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) Ltd. \par \par \uc2  The government's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the markets, added to the effects of \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recession, could cut foreign reserves to as little as $20 billion by the end of 1999, the report said. That would rattle public confidence in the economy and the Hong Kong dollar, the report warned. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  declined to say if it had bought shares in September. The quasi-\b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in August bought billions of dollars in local shares in an effort to squeeze speculators out of the market. \par \par \uc2  Stock traders, however, have said throughout September that they saw few signs of government buying. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Pan Ming, Prudential-Bache's equities strategist, said in the report\par that the government understated its share buying in its foreign reserve\par figures for August.\par    The government said reserves at the end of August stood at $92.1\par billion, down from $96.5 billion at the end of July. The government\par attributed the decline to both share buying and spending. In addition,\par it acknowledged holding unsettled \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  contracts, which,\par once settled, would cut reserves to $87.6 billion, Pan said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par However, Pan said, total share-buying had to be greater, because the\par government now holds about 8.9% of HSBC Holdings PLC., the market's\par biggest capitalized stock. Pan estimates the government spent $5.3\par billion on HSBC shares. He puts total spending on shares at $15 billion\par to $20 billion.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 30-09-98\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 0434GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du9u08ew5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
