\par\par \b \uc2  HK firms tolerant of government  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2  By Jennifer Genevieve \par \uc2 686 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 September 1998\par 11:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 HONG KONG\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Sept 29 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 's response to Asia's economic crisis has stirred controversy, but some businesses said they were willing to look beyond government infringement of free market principles to long-term prospects offered by China. \par \par \uc2  "It does not make me any less confident in Hong Kong and China and it does not make me want to move our business away. It does not make me particularly nervous," said Gary Coull, chairman of Hong Kong-based Credit Lyonnais Securities Asia. \par \par \uc2  "I just think you have to be aware of it," he said. \par \par \uc2  Government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in both the property and financial markets have sullied \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 's free market reputation, drawing criticism at home and abroad. \par \par \uc2  In a two-week \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaign in the stock market last month, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  spent an estimated HK$100 billion (US$12.9 billion) to squeeze out "manipulators" who were active in Hong Kong's stock and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong General Chamber of Commerce, which has about 4,000 corporate members, said while the move was unusual for Hong Kong, it did not detract from people's views on investment here and the future business climate. \par \par \uc2  "In reviewing exactly what happened, there was general support and sympathy because the business community is very much behind maintaining the Hong Kong dollar peg and realised that this was an emergency measure," director Eden Woon said. \par \par \uc2  "Hong Kong is still the most international of all Asian cities and a place where a foreign investor will find the most comfort both in terms of regulations and in terms of the ease of doing business and living. And plus the proximity of China which nobody is going to be able to duplicate," Woon said. \par \par \uc2  It was the stability of the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  which, from the trade point of view, would keep businesses here, said Edward Leung, chief economist at the Hong Kong Trade Development Council. \par \par \uc2  "Exchange rate stability is most important to them and Hong Kong will continue to maintain a liberal trading regime," he said. \par \par \uc2  Measures announced early this month to tighten regulations in the securities markets would leave Hong Kong's position as a regional hub for fund management unharmed, said Desmond Chan, chairman of the Hong Kong Investment Funds Association. \par \par \uc2  "What has happened lately I don't think is enough to make fund managers desert Hong Kong," said Chan, pointing to local regulations and the tax regime. \par \par \uc2  "Hong Kong will be a gateway to China in some future time so if someone has an eye on China's fund management industry in years to come, it may be better to have an office here." \par \par \uc2  The hinterland has given Hong Kong an advantage which helps offset the higher costs of doing business here compared with other countries where \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have depreciated, SG Securities said in a recent report. \par \par \uc2  "We dispute the simple-minded thinking that HK has fundamentally lost its competitiveness to the point where only a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluation can restore that competitiveness," it said. \par \par \uc2  "A regional service centre's competitiveness is NOT, repeat NOT, determined overwhelmingly by the level of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," the report said. \par \par \uc2  Mike Rowse, director of the Hong Kong government's business and services promotion unit, said businesses were not just looking at price, but also at what else was offered here. \par \par \uc2  "You have the banks, you have the lawyers, you have the accountants, you have the chartered secretaries, you have the whole shebang," he told Reuters in an interview. \par \par \uc2  While concerns about the government's recent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would fade away, businesses would focus on the free movement of capital, rule of law, freedom of the press and freedom of movement available in the territory, Rowse said. \par \par \uc2  "We have got virtually all the big things right which is why we feel slightly embittered that there has been a spillover from the places that did not get these things right," he said. \par \par \uc2  ((Hong Kong Newsroom (852) 2843 6345; Fax (852) 2845 0636)) \par \par \uc2  ((Email: hongkong.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021108du9t023k8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
