\par\par \b \uc2  HKMA's Yam Will Skip IMF Meetings To Monitor Local Mkts \b0\par\par\uc2 174 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 September 1998\par 10:43\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG (Dow Jones)--In light of continuing concerns about financial markets, Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief, Joseph Yam Chi-kwong, has decided not to attend this year's annual meetings of the World Bank and International Monetary Fund in Washington. \par \par \uc2  "He will not go," said a spokesman for the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 , where Yam is chief executive. "Joseph will remain in Hong Kong to monitor the markets," as well as the effectiveness of technical changes made to the territory's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system over the past month, according to the spokesman on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Financial Secretary Donald Tsang Yam-kuen, Yam's boss, will lead the Hong Kong delegation. Tsang has been urged by some in the community to arrange a meeting with Federal Reserve Board Chairman Alan Greenspan in response to criticism leveled by the U.S. official about the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  government's heavy financial market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the past month. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 25-09-98 \par \par \uc2  0843GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du9p08a8b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
