\par\par \b \uc2  Hong Kong stocks close up on technical rebound. \b0\par\par\uc2 905 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 1998\par 12:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, Sept 22 (Reuters) - Hong Kong stocks shrugged off three straight days of declines to finish higher on Tuesday on a technical rebound triggered by firmer U.S. and Tokyo stocks, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  The Hang Seng Index ended up 203.28 points, or 2.84 percent, at 7,373.51, but was off the day's high of 7,438.33 points. \par \par \uc2  "The rally on Hong Kond stocks is mainly due to a rebound on Wall Street in late trade overnight after people found that Clinton's video tapes seemed to be not that devastating," said Alex Tang, research director at Core Pacific - Yamaichi International (HK). \par \par \uc2  The Dow industrials erased an 185-point early loss to close at 7,933.25, up 37.59 points, or 0.48 percent on Monday as the release of U.S. President Bill Clinton's videotaped grand jury testimony was considered less damaging to him than feared. \par \par \uc2  "The Hong Kong market took a plunge yesterday ahead of the U.S. market and the U.S. did not really fall a lot last night so there is a rebound today," said Frederick Tsang, director at DBS Securities. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo's Nikkei 225 average finished up 192.51 points, or 1.42 percent, at 13,789.81 on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  But the Japanese yen weakened to 135 to the U.S. dollar in late trade, triggering light profit-taking in Hong Kong stocks, Tang said. The yen opened in Asia on Tuesday at 134.44/49. \par \par \uc2  "A slip on the yen fuelled concerns about the outlook of the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  since a weak yen would put renewed pressure on the Hong Kong dollar," Tang said. \par \par \uc2  The market was also watching the development of a political wrangling between the Japanese government and opposition parties which held up crucial financial reform bills, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  Utilities led the blue chip gainers with CLP Holdings  gaining HK$1.20 to HK$35.90, Hongkong Electric  up HK$1.15 at HK$26.50 and Hongkong Telecom  rising HK$0.50 to HK$14.80. \par \par \uc2  HSBC Holdings  also added HK$3.50 to HK$135.50. \par \par \uc2  Hutchison  advanced HK$1.10 to HK$36.40. Hutchison Telecom, a unit of Hutchison Whampoa Ltd, said on Tuesday it would announce a new plan for Hutchison Telecom's mobile \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a news conference on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Hutchison Whampoa said last week it was launching a new marketing strategy to promote the group's telecommunications business, including advertising for British-based mobile phone arm Orange Plc . It declined to give more details. \par \par \uc2  Shangri-La Asia  jumped HK$0.65, or 15.76 percent, to close at HK$4.775. \par \par \uc2  September index futures added 275 points to 7,395, while the October contract rose 270 points to 7,140 and December futures added 260 points to 7,100. \par \par \uc2  "The September future is trading quite close to the index. I think there is some switching activity. The short sellers are covering September positions and switching into October ones," Tsang said. \par \par \uc2  Paliburg  fell HK$0.30, or 12.24 percent, to HK$2.15, weighed by a recent sharp price fall of Chi Cheung . Chi Cheung shares dropped 87.6 percent last Friday before a trading suspension. \par \par \uc2  Paliburg and Chi Cheung chairman Paul Ng formed a joint venture, Rapid Growth Holdings Ltd, to hold a 74.06 percent stake in Chi Cheung. \par \par \uc2  China plays were firmer. The red chip Hang Seng China-Affiliated Corporations Index added 15.88 points, or 2.21 percent, to 734.42 and the Hang Seng Chinese Enterprises Index rose 13.75 points, or 4.07 percent, to 351.44 points. \par \par \uc2  Brokers said Hong Kong stocks lacked direction and should move within a range of 7,000 to 7,800 with investors looking to overseas markets for trading clues. \par \par \uc2  "There are still investors who are confident that when the HSI corrects itself, there is still value in holding onto Hong Kong stocks," said Vivian Kwok, head of research at Sassoon Securities. \par \par \uc2  "There is value at 7,000. When that level is reached I think there will still be a lot of buyers," she added. \par \par \uc2  Brokers said there was little reaction to the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 's (HKMA) announcement on Monday that foreign reserves had fallen to US$92.1 billion at end-August from US$96.5 billion at end-July. \par \par \uc2  The drop in reserves could be attributed to both fiscal drawdowns by the government treasury and the government's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the stock and futures market, the HKMA said. \par \par \uc2  The authority would not reveal exactly how much was spent on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , citing the government's desire to keep such information from speculators. \par \par \uc2  But the HKMA did say US$4.4 billion of the Exchange Fund was sold on the last two trading days on August 28 and 31 to fund investment in Hong Kong stocks. Those transactions would be settled in September. \par \par \uc2  Market analysts said the government's heftiest purchases were made at the end of August, when turnover jumped to a record shattering HK$79 billion on August 28. \par \par \uc2  Brokers noted that part of the funding of the stock \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would have come from \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar reserves, so it was difficult to determine how much money was spent. \par \par \uc2  - Alison Leung (852) 2843 6369; Fax (852) 2845 0636 \par \par \uc2  - Email: hongkong.newsroom@reuters.com. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021108du9m01ofm\par