\par\par \b \uc2  Hong Kong stocks lower in quiet morning trade. \b0\par\par\uc2 518 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 1998\par 05:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, Sept 17 (Reuters) - Hong Kong stocks were lower on Thursday morning in quiet trade on profit-taking after a recent rally in China plays ran out of steam, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  The Hang Seng Index was down 132 points, or 1.69 percent, at 7,727 points. Turnover was HK$1.09 billion. \par \par \uc2  "Red chips and H shares are starting to correct following a strong rally earlier this month," said Ricky Tam, senior research analyst at Delta Asia Securities. \par \par \uc2  The Hang Seng Chinese Enterprises Index of H shares fell 13 points, or 3.36 percent, to 370 points and the red chip index lost 20 points, or 2.45 percent to 791 points. \par \par \uc2  "It is natural for the sectors to head for a correction since they have risen too fast too sharply," said Peter Lai, director at OCBC Securities. \par \par \uc2  But the Shougang group of companies were firmer ahead of their interim results due later on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Shougang International  added HK$0.015, or 4.11 percent, to HK$0.38, Shougang Technology  jumped HK$0.025, or 7.81 percent, to HK$0.345 and Shougang Grand  advanced HK$0.01, or 2.38 percent, to HK$0.43. \par \par \uc2  CNPC (Hong Kong)  surged HK$0.05, or 6.67 percent, to HK$0.80. The company will announce its interim results this afternoon. \par \par \uc2  HSBC Holdings  remained weak, easing HK$2.00 to HK$146.00. \par \par \uc2  Cheung Kong lost HK$0.90, or 2.62 percent, to HK$33.50. The developer said on Wednesday it would launch 24 units at its Tai Po residential development at an average of HK$3,468 per square foot, below their cost of more than HK$4,000 per square foot. \par \par \uc2  "This could be a sales strategy but even Cheung Kong has to sell flats at a loss. The outlook for the property market seems to be bleak," Lai said. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan's criticism of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  government's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the financial markets also affected sentiment, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  Greenspan said on Wednesday Hong Kong's efforts to boost share prices by using reserves to buy stock could not succeed and would damage the credibility of the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  September futures lost 180 points at 7,570 and October eased 190 to 7,390 points. \par \par \uc2  But trading was slow and thin ahead of provisional unemployment data for the June to August period to be announced later on Thursday. The market expected the jobless figure to rise to 5.1 to 5.2 percent compared to provisional 4.8 percent for May-July. \par \par \uc2  "Investors are waiting for the job data and a panel meeting of the Legislative Council for trading clues," Tam said. \par \par \uc2  Financial Secretary Donald Tsang and HKMA Chief Executive Joseph Yam will attend a Financial Affairs panel meeting this afternoon to explain recent actions by the government in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , stock and futures markets. \par \par \uc2  - Alison Leung (852) 2843 6369; Fax (852) 2845 0636 \par \par \uc2  - Email: hongkong.newsroom@reuters.com *. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021108du9h018r5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
