\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong \highlight\uc2  Again Defends  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Aimed At Supporting  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 588 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 1998\par 20:57\par \uc2  Dow Jones Online News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Financial Secretary again defended his government's unprecedented \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in stock and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets last month and former governor Christopher Patten Thursday said Hong Kong remains determined to preserve the link between the Hong Kong dollar and the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong government has accused traders of improperly manipulating both its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and stock markets in an attempt to sever the dollar link and force a devaluation of the Hong Kong dollar. \par \par \uc2  The deteriorating state of the global economy made Hong Kong's campaign to support its market extremely costly. Traders estimate the government spent between $12 billion and $15 billion on shares in a two week period last month. The government - through the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  - used \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to buy blue-chip stocks, Hang Seng Index futures and the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  More recently, Hong Kong introduced restrictions on certain types of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and futures trading. Analysts believe the actions have exposed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market's vulnerabilities. Critics say the moves have provided speculators with exactly the kind of tactical information they need to try something new. \par \par \uc2  Responding to criticism from Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan, \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  Financial Secretary Donald Tsang Thursday said the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was justified and necessary. "We were in circumstances that we believed we had absolutely no other option," Tsang said. \par \par \uc2  Greenspan Wednesday said Asian economies shouldn't turn their backs on open markets. He singled out the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  government's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for criticism. "I don't think it can succeed," Greenspan said. "The consequences of doing that erodes some of the extraordinary credibility that the Hong Kong monetary authorities have achieved over the years." the freest market in the world." \par \par \uc2  "We do understand that from an idealistic position of a free market, what we did is probably rather exceptional," Tsang claimed that other members of the Fed expressed support for the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, PAtten said China won't devalue its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , because it's not in its economic interest to do so. "As far as the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is concerned, they've got a good deal of credit, and I think that's to a certain extent justified for what they've said about not devaluing," Patten said. Patten also said he suspects that China's "dollar indebtedness is much greater than the official figures suggest, so (a devaluation) would greatly put up the cost of that," said Patten. \par \par \uc2  "As far as the Hong Kong dollar is concerned, the present government will be as determined as I was when we were attacked from time to time to preserve the link with the U.S. dollar, because it's the basis of Hong Kong's demonstration of financial autonomy and independence," he said. \par \par \uc2  China has loudly pledged it won't devalue its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yuan, or the Hong Kong dollar anytime soon. Although rumors continue to make the rounds in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and stock markets around the world that China may devalue the yuan, the People's Bank of China a few weeks ago proclaimed China won't do so. \par \par \uc2  Many people fear that a devalued yuan would further batter Asia's depreciated \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , spreading the region's economic dislocation to the West. Experts say the pledge has teeth but investors and traders were burned many times in the past year as one Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after another collapsed despite assurances of stability. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1998 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020329du9h018z7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
