\par\par \b \uc2  Institutions fuel Hong Kong's China-play rally. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Alison Leung \par \uc2 624 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 1998\par 12:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, Sept 16 (Reuters) - A return of institutional interest in China-related shares is fuelling a powerful rally in red chips and H-shares amid optimism that China's economy will pick up and interest rates will fall, market analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "Fund managers are buying these stocks since China's economic environment seems to have improved slightly and there are hopes that China will cut interest rates soon," said Peter So, a China analyst at Indosuez W.I. Carr Securities. \par \par \uc2  He said fund managers also wanted to lower their cash positions ahead of the end of the quarter. \par \par \uc2  The Hang Seng Chinese Enterprises Index jumped 26.85 points, or 7.53 percent, to close at 383.33 on Wednesday and the red chip Hang Seng China-Affiliated Corporations Index added 28.20 points, or 3.60 percent, to 810.90 points. \par \par \uc2  The H-share and red chip indices have leapt 59.5 percent and 40.2 percent each so far this month. \par \par \uc2  With recent trading thin, brokers said institutional buying had easily pushed up prices. The rallies were sharp partly because the China-related sector had been oversold after foreign funds cleared out their holdings earlier this year, So said. \par \par \uc2  The H-share index is still 50.1 percent below its 1998 high of 767.53 points and the red chip index is 56.43 percent lower than its high of 1,860.98 points this year. \par \par \uc2  H-shares are the shares of China-incorporated enterprises listed in Hong Kong. Red chips are Hong Kong-based companies with mainland Chinese backing. \par \par \uc2  "These sectors had been sold down indiscriminately and now they are being bought up indiscriminately," said Kalina Ip, head of China research at ABN Amro Asia. \par \par \uc2  A strengthening yen had eased fears of a depreciation of the Chinese yuan, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  But Andrew Fernow, director of research at Vickers Ballas, said fundamentals had not changed and that the rallies were purely due to short covering. \par \par \uc2  "Be careful because I think given the breadth of the rally and the momentum, it could easily subside after the short-covering is completed. There may not be as much momentum to carry these groups of stocks much higher," he said. \par \par \uc2  The earnings picture for the sector was still quite disturbing, said Fernow. \par \par \uc2  "There are a few exceptions, like the road companies and some of the food companies, which have announced satisfactory (interim) results," he added. \par \par \uc2  Ip said once an interest rate cut materialised, profit taking would emerge to trim gains on the stocks with poor earnings outlooks. \par \par \uc2  Wang Jiachun, a researcher at China's Finance Ministry, said China had few new measures left to stimulate its slowing economy in the near term with the exception of a cut in bank interest rates, the Shanghai Securities newspaper reported last Friday. \par \par \uc2  He did not say that an interest rate cut was imminent but rumours have circulated in Hong Kong and China's financial markets that Beijing will soon cut bank interest rates for the third time this year. \par \par \uc2  So said the rally could last for a while since fund managers had not seemed to have completed their buy programmes yet. \par \par \uc2  "Buying does not seem to have been completed yet since the cash level of foreign funds remains high after they sold large amounts of blue chips to the government during the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in August," he said. \par \par \uc2  Brokers estimate that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  spent about US$15 billion during a two-week \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaign in the stock and futures markets in August to foil \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators. \par \par \uc2  - Hong Kong newsroom (852) 2843 6369; Fax (852) 2845 0636 \par \par \uc2  - Email: hongkong.newsroom@reuters.com. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021108du9g019s1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
