\par\par \b \uc2  HK stocks end up but off highs, China plays lead. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Kristina Zetterlund \par \uc2 525 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 1998\par 11:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, Sept 15 (Reuters) - Hong Kong stocks closed moderately higher on Tuesday, encouraged by a strong performance by China-related stocks and after Wall Street kicked off the week on a strong note, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  The Hang Seng Index gained 71.61 points, or 0.93 percent, to finish at 7,733.47. It earlier hit a high of 7,837.96 before running into profit-taking. \par \par \uc2  China plays took centre stage on Tuesday, boosted by talk about a possible Chinese interest rate cut in the near future, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  China's increased investment in infrastructure and reconstruction plans after floods would provide fuel to stoke China's economy in the second half of 1998, one broker said. \par \par \uc2  The "red chip" index added 2.79 percent to 782.70 while H shares gained 5.99 percent to 356.48. \par \par \uc2  Ricky Tam, senior research analyst at Delta Asia Securities, said some investors may be offloading their holdings ahead of a possible sell-off of China plays if China does deliver a rate cut. \par \par \uc2  Brokers expected the market to remain relatively stable the rest of the week with the focus on Wall Street and U.S. President Bill Clinton's political future. \par \par \uc2  Investors also waited for clues from Tokyo's stock market, which was closed on Tuesday for a holiday. \par \par \uc2  "If some local investors already missed this train, then they don't want to buy at this moment. They want to wait for the market to come down," Tam said. \par \par \uc2  Tam added foreign investors were staying on the sidelines, wary after the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  government's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the financial markets last month. \par \par \uc2  The Hang Seng Index was expected to face resistance at 8,000 with support found at 7,500, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  Market focus was on the next meeting of the U.S. Federal Reserve on September 29, brokers said. Percy Au-Young, sales director at DBS Securities, said a U.S. interest rate cut may trigger a rate cut in Hong Kong. \par \par \uc2  "I think Hong Kong is very eager to cut its interest rates," said Au-Young. \par \par \uc2  Interbank rates eased on Tuesday after the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it would hold to its undertaking to convert the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at HK$7.75 per U.S. dollar for at least six months. \par \par \uc2  The benchmark three-month HIBOR rate dropped to 10.12500 percent at Tuesday's fixing against 11.89375 percent at the same time on Monday. \par \par \uc2  HSBC Holdings lagged, dropping HK$3.50 to HK$147.00 after hitting a year low of HK$145.50. Brokers said the share price was weighed down by worries about the banking giant's exposure to a weakening global economy. \par \par \uc2  "A lot of people continue to doubt about its (HSBC Holdings') evaluations, especially after seeing European banks falling in the latest round of correction," said Au-Young. \par \par \uc2  Au-Young believed fair value for HSBC Holdings was currently at HK$137 to HK$142. \par \par \uc2  Tam said worries about HSBC Holdings' exposure to Brazil added to pressure on the stock. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021108du9f0157d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
