\par\par \b \uc2  CORRECTED - Hong Kong fight against speculators fuels jingoism. \b0\par\par\uc2 703 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 September 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  In HONG KONG story headlined "Hong Kong fight against speculators fuels jingoism" please read in sixth paragraph ....On Saturday, Rebecca Lai, acting secretary for financial services, will lead the government's third appearance this week .... instead of ....Financial Secretary Donald Tsang will appear for the third time this week (correcting name). \par \par \uc2  A corrected story follows. \par \par \uc2  By Tan Ee Lyn \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, Sept 11 (Reuters) - The Hong Kong government's crusade against market speculators has become a rallying cry, fuelling a sense of nationalism once rare in this former British colony, political analysts and legislators said on Friday. \par \par \uc2  But some warned that such "jingoism" will harm the image of this international financial powerhouse in the long run and drive investors away. \par \par \uc2  Government officials "are creating an impression for the general public in Hong Kong that there are speculators that are not so bad, manipulators who are really bad and these are essentially foreign devils," said legislator Christine Loh. \par \par \uc2  "To divide market players into good ones and bad ones, and the bad ones are from the outside is just not what we need. I mean what impression are we creating internationally?" said Loh, head of the Citizens' Party and a former commodities trader. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong has locked horns in recent weeks with what it has termed "market manipulators" who are trying to profit from attacking the U.S.-pegged Hong Kong dollar. \par \par \uc2  On Saturday, Rebecca Lai, acting secretary for financial services, will lead the government's third appearance this week before the legislature's Financial Affairs panel to defend the government's actions, a government spokeswoman said. \par \par \uc2  Since mid-August, the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  has spent at least HK$100 billion (US$12.9 billion) in the local stock and futures markets to foil speculators it accused of selling off the Hong Kong dollar to drive interest rates up and stock prices down to take profits on short stock positions. \par \par \uc2  In the past week the government has announced 37 new measures to strengthen the Hong Kong dollar's link to the U.S. dollar and make markets less susceptible to manipulation. \par \par \uc2  The moves, under full glare of the local media, have been hotly debated by legislators in this week's earlier sessions with Financial Secretary Donald Tsang. \par \par \uc2  Many politicians have used the forum to unleash wild and colourful invectives against foreign "manipulators" - not unlike the rhetoric that Malaysian Prime Minister Mahathir Mohamad has used for months to denounce \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators. \par \par \uc2  James Tien of the Liberal Party referred to them as "gweilos", a Cantonese term for "foreign devils", while Chan Kam-lam of the pro-China Democratic Alliance of the Betterment of Hong Kong, slammed them as "international big crocodiles." \par \par \uc2  Amid the name calling, Tsang, the top general in \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 's market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaign, has won growing support among the territory's 6.6 million people, surveys showed. \par \par \uc2  A poll by the University of Hong Kong on September 7 found 54.9 percent of 548 respondents agreed with the government's recent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in stocks and futures, up from 37.4 percent in an earlier survey conducted on September 1. \par \par \uc2  Tsang's approval rating scored an all-time high of 65.1 percent in the same poll. His rating stood at 58.1 percent in August 1998 and 61.3 percent in April 1997. \par \par \uc2  Political analyst Lau Siu-kai said the support was because of a groundswell of "anti-speculator sentiment." \par \par \uc2  Legislator Margaret Ng said the support given to the government was based on "financial jingoism" and warned that it could encourage the government to do whatever it chooses. \par \par \uc2  "The greater the support the government can count on from the jingoists, the greater will be the difficulty for Legco (Legislative Council) to act as check and balance in holding the government to its legal restraints," Ng wrote in a commentary in the South China Morning Post on Friday. \par \par \uc2  As an indicator of how the international community may be reacting to \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves, U.S. investment house Goldman Sachs on Friday said it was cutting its Hong Kong recommendation to underweight from neutral, while lifting Singapore to overweight from underweight. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021108du9b00wei\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
