\par\par \b \uc2  Funds wary of HK stocks, wait for dust to settle. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Jennifer Genevieve \par \uc2 681 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 September 1998\par 11:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 HONG KONG\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Sept 9 (Reuters) - The \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  government's massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the local stock market, and subsequent plans to tighten trading rules, has left fund managers wary of the market as they wait for the dust to settle. \par \par \uc2  "Right now there is still a lot of confusion and difficulties in terms of long-term investors positioning themselves in Hong Kong," said John Lai, chief investment officer at Nikko Global Asset Management (Hong Kong). \par \par \uc2  "Long-term funds will obviously wait for a while because I think a lot of them have exited the market since the government stepped in to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  and fundamentals are departing from stock prices," he said. \par \par \uc2  The government is estimated to have spent some HK$100 billion (US$12.9 billion) in the stock market last month in a two-week buying spree designed to chase out speculators who had sold the Hong Kong dollar and pushed interest rates higher while shorting the stock market to profit from falling share prices. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong Financial Secretary Donald Tsang followed up this week with proposals to tighten securities rules. \par \par \uc2  The Hang Seng Index is up 19 percent since \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  began on August 14. On Wednesday, it closed down 283.80 points, or 3.47 percent, at 7,905.45. \par \par \uc2  "The market remains very volatile and when you have volatility you have caution," said Philip Hampden-Smith, chief executive officer at Manulife Funds Direct (HK) Ltd. \par \par \uc2  "While we have seen some good numbers coming out of the performance of the market over the last two or three days, I think the problems are by no means over." \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong is facing its first full-year recession on record, with the government expecting the economy to shrink by about four percent this year. Unemployment is on a rising trend. \par \par \uc2  Interbank rates have dropped substantially since the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  implemented measures on Monday to boost liquidity in the banking system and strengthen the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board. But the trend needs to continue before any benefits are seen with prime rates coming down, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "The economic fundamentals are still far from sound and Asia is going through what I consider a very sharp recession and it is going to take at least a year or two," said Terence Mahony, managing director at TCW Asia. \par \par \uc2  "We are underweight and if it corrects we will probably look to act but I still think cash is king at the moment." \par \par \uc2  Provided interest rates remained stable, the equilibrium level for the Hang Seng Index was around 6,500 to 7,000 points and a drop to lower levels could prompt some buying, Mahony said. \par \par \uc2  "I would definitely be a nibbler all things being equal. I think you have to look at the external factors too." \par \par \uc2  Stewart Aldcroft, marketing director at Templeton Franklin Investment Services (Asia), said Hong Kong was offering good value on a five-year view but more bad news for the region could lie around the corner. \par \par \uc2  "The economic situation in Asia continues to remain in a very volatile state with uncertainty in many different areas so by no means can I say in any way that we have yet gone through the worst of it," he said. \par \par \uc2  "There are lots more things that are sitting on the horizon, many of them we probably just don't know about yet, but they keep coming up. Something turns up when you are not looking." \par \par \uc2  While the government has claimed victory in its fight against speculators and has taken action to make financial markets more resilient to attacks, the Hong Kong dollar may be tested again, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "Any news can again trigger excuses to come back and look at our \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board arrangement," said Lai. \par \par \uc2  "The risk premium of Hong Kong is unlikely to come down in the near term for equity investors," he said. \par \par \uc2  (US$1 = HK$7.75) \par \par \uc2  ((Hong Kong Newsroom (852) 2843 6345; Fax (852) 2845 0636)) \par \par \uc2  ((Email: hongkong.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021108du9900qbu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
