\par\par \b \uc2  HK dlr still seen vulnerable despite near-term relief. \b0\par\par\uc2  By David Lawder \par \uc2 737 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 September 1998\par 15:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, Sept 8 (Reuters) - The Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 's package of reforms will limit market manipulation and stabilize near-term interest rates, but bankers said Tuesday the territory may remain a juicy target for speculators. \par \par \uc2  "The perceptions that Hong Kong is not competitive and the perceptions that Hong Kong assets are relatively overvalued are not going away because of these measures," said Stephen Yau, treasurer at ING Barings. "Speculators may still look to attack Hong Kong on this basis." \par \par \uc2  The measures to strengthen Hong Kong's U.S.-linked exchange rate succeeded in holding down interest rates for the second day in a row, as the three-month benchmark interbank rate closed at 8.37 percent on Tuesday versus last Friday's 11 percent close. \par \par \uc2  Bankers continued to digest the HKMA's sweeping seven-point plan announced on Saturday, but said it went a long way toward boosting confidence in the territory's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system, which pegs the Hong Kong dollar to the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  Two of the most important reforms were the HKMA's undertaking to convert Hong Kong dollars in licensed banks' clearing accounts to U.S. dollars at a fixed exchange rate of HK$7.75 per US$1 and the conversion of its old Liquidity Adjustment Facility (LAF) to a discount window. \par \par \uc2  The convertibility undertaking shows the HKMA's firm commitment to the peg system and gives banks a guaranteed exchange rate of HK$7.75 to US$1, said Andrew Fung, treasurer of the Commonwealth Bank of Australia. \par \par \uc2  "They're fully committed to the 7.75 figure," Fung said. "Speculators can't spread any rumors on the peg anymore." \par \par \uc2  The guaranteed rate also creates an arbitrage opportunity for banks should the Hong Kong dollar come under heavy selling pressure that weakens the market exchange rate. Since banks can always sell their Hong Kong dollars at the end of the day to the HKMA at HK$7.75 to US$1, this will encourage them to buy Hong Kong dollars in the market, helping to stabilize rates. \par \par \uc2  "Under these new measures, we don't have to be reliant on interest rate arbitrage to bring the exchange rate close to the official level," said Roger Luk, deputy chief executive of Hong Kong's Hang Seng Bank. "You can use the exchange rate itself." \par \par \uc2  But the downside of the more direct arbitrage is that the HKMA, Hong Kong's de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , may have to use more of its US$96.5 billion in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to defend the local dollar, said Yau of ING Barings. \par \par \uc2  The new discount window will improve interbank liquidity because it does not have the same restrictions on repeated borrowings as the LAF did. Those restrictions discouraged banks from using the LAF, leading to interest rate spikes during periods of heavy selling of Hong Kong dollars. \par \par \uc2  HKMA officials argued that the moves make the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board less vulnerable to speculative attacks because it is more difficult for speculators to affect interest rates, and hence the stock market by selling \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollars. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  government launched a massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the local stock and futures markets in mid-August, aiming to foil speculators it said were attacking the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to drive interest rates up and stock prices down to profit from short stock positions. \par \par \uc2  "These measures make the speculator's life very difficult and it's very good for short term sentiment" said Commonwealth's Fung. "I dont' think specultors can succeed in the short term. In the long term, I don't know." \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index has gained 12 percent over the last three trading days, advancing 112.49 points or 1.39 percent on Tuesday to close at 8,189.25 on the eased local interbank rates. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong's slumping economy, Asia's continuing financial turmoil and other external factors will continue to put pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system, bankers said, until an economic recovery or a change in sentiment takes hold. \par \par \uc2  "Economic adjustment will still go on," Hang Seng's Luk said. "These measures will ensure we have more stable interest rates and narrower spreads, and that will make the adjustment easier." \par \par \uc2  ((Hong Kong newsroom (852) 2843 6321 Fax (852) 2845 0636 Email: hongkong.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021217du9800yfr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
