\par\par \b \uc2  AWSJ: HK Battles Speculators; Patten Plays Down  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 612 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 September 1998\par 03:24\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  From Tuesday's Asian Wall Street Journal \par \par \uc2  By G. Bruce Knecht and Erik Guyot \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG (Dow Jones)--The government appears to have gained ground, at least temporarily, in its war against speculators accused of assailing the stability of local financial markets. \par \par \uc2  During the weekend, officials proposed several rule changes intended to make it more difficult to pressure Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , stock and futures markets. Monday, as Financial Secretary Donald Tsang made his first appearance before legislators since the government's unprecedented \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last month into the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  stock market, share prices surged. The Hang Seng Index rose 7.9% to close at 8076.76. \par \par \uc2  Chris Patten, the last British governor of \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 , disagreed with those who claim \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will do lasting damage to the city's reputation as a financial center. "I don't think it changes, or should change, investors' fundamental view of Hong Kong," he said by telephone in his first comments on the local economy since Britain's colonial rule ended July 1, 1997. \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  "clearly represents a departure from the previous approach," Patten said. But he added: "Presumably the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  knew this would have an impact on Hong Kong's reputation" and took that impact into consideration. \par \par \uc2  Patten did join the chorus criticizing the government's refusal to disclose the amount of shares it accumulated during the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "Everywhere in Asia the crisis has demonstrated the need for increased transparency and more openness in economic affairs," he said. \par \par \uc2  The government bought shares in all 33 companies making up the Hang Seng Index, and HSBC Holdings PLC last week disclosed that Hong Kong owns 8.9% of its shares. The government says it is exempt from a local rule requiring disclosure of such holdings. "If we disclose the amount now it will only help others, not ourselves," Secretary Tsang said. \par \par \uc2  Patten disagreed: "It's plainly a central feature of the rule of law that everyone is subject to it." \par \par \uc2  Tsang also described the rule changes intended to moderate swings in interbank and other short-term interest rates by increasing liquidity in the banking system. \par \par \uc2  The government's moves are aimed at preventing speculators from making what officials call a "double play," in which they reap big profits by simultaneously attacking the Hong Kong dollar and selling short Hong Kong shares or futures contracts. Under Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  system, attacks on the Hong Kong dollar automatically push up interest rates, which slam corporate profits and share prices. In a short sale, an investor borrows stock and then sells it in the expectation that the price will fall, enabling him to buy it back more cheaply before returning it to the lender. \par \par \uc2  Joseph Yam, chief executive of the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said that as a result of the new measures, speculators "would need to sell quite a lot more (Hong Kong dollars) to make interest rates go up." \par \par \uc2  Such mechanisms might make it harder for speculators to profit by reducing market volatility, but they don't necessarily address a more fundamental problem: the lack of confidence in the Hong Kong dollar's peg to the U.S. dollar, which has helped create a crippling credit crunch and aggravated the city's worst recession in 30 years. Tsang said the government remains committed to the 15-year-old peg that fixes the value of the Hong Kong dollar at 7.8 to the U.S. dollar. The government won't impose the kind of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls that were implemented by Malaysia last week, he added. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 08-09-98 \par \par \uc2  0124GMT \par \par \uc2  (Corrected 0410GMT) \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du9807oou\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
