\par\par \b \uc2  HK says  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  sparked by worst crisis since '83. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Kathleen Kearney \par \uc2 723 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 September 1998\par 17:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 HONG KONG\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Sept 7 (Reuters) - Speculative attacks on the Hong Kong dollar emerged just before the government launched its massive stock market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last month, officials said on Monday. \par \par \uc2  "We knew in the second week or rather first week of August that we are dealing with a very serious crisis, almost in line with the situation we saw in October 1983, when we went into the linked exchange rate," Financial Secretary Donald Tsang told reporters at a briefing, referring to the introduction of the Hong Kong dollars' peg against the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  The briefing following the administration's presentation to the Financial Affairs Panel of the Legislative Council on its unprecedented \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the stock market. \par \par \uc2  The financial secretary refused to tell legislators or reporters the total amount the administration spent in buying stock and index futures, but market estimates put it at more than HK$100 billion. \par \par \uc2  Legislative councillors will continue their review into the administration's actions on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong pegged its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the dollar at a rate of HK$7.80 to the dollar in October 1983, when the pace of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's year-long decline quickened and sent it plunging past the rate of nine to one. \par \par \uc2  Speculation against the Hong Kong dollar had been seen in the wake of adjustments in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 1997 and early 1998, but never on this scale, Tsang said. \par \par \uc2  "What we saw in the middle or early part of August was vastly different from what we had in the previous few occasions," Tsang said. \par \par \uc2  "There was hardly any coordination of activities during the attack of last October," Tsang said. At that time the Hang Seng Index declined sharply and activity in the Hong Kong dollar forwards market increased. \par \par \uc2  Interbank interest rates spiked higher briefly, but were returned to relatively normal levels with an injection of liquidity from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Hang Seng Index was still trading above the 10,000, and turnover was in the region of HK$30 billion, Tsang said, adding turnover in Hang Seng Index futures was roughly 60,000 to 70,000 contracts. \par \par \uc2  "The market was pretty large for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  raiders to do a double act. It was a lot more difficult." \par \par \uc2  The double act consists of shorting the Hong Kong dollar forcing interest rates higher while holding short positions in the stock and futures markets. \par \par \uc2  A build-up in open Hang Seng Index futures positions is the most obvious sign of growing bearishness on the stock market or \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  or both. \par \par \uc2  Another attack on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was seen in January this year, Tsang said. But market conditions were similar to those in October and the market regained some, albeit weak, equilibrium. \par \par \uc2  "But what we saw in august - the Hang Seng Index had fallen to 6,600, turnover was HK$4 to HK$5.0 billion, but what was most worrying was the open interest in Hang Seng Futures had risen to 111,000. In fact, there was a jump of 10,000 within three trading days," Tsang said. \par \par \uc2  A pattern of speculation against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was seen, he said. \par \par \uc2  "There was a very deliberate and very calculated act which attacked us in the New York market, followed through with Sydney, then Hong Kong, then followed up in London," Tsang said. \par \par \uc2  "The pattern was the same day in day out." \par \par \uc2  In addition, the volume of Hong Kong dollars "dumped" was a much greater quantity than Hong Kong authorities had seen before. \par \par \uc2  Other shaky global markets, including Russia and markets in Latin America, were also adding fuel to the speculative fire. \par \par \uc2  "So everything put together we knew that something serious was going to happen if we sat on our hands," Tsang said. \par \par \uc2  On August 27, total turnover on the Hong Kong stock market rose to HK$22.97 billion and on August 28 to a record-shattering HK$79.0 billion, a very large portion of which is attributed to the government buying. \par \par \uc2  "It has been a very difficult decision for us and we did not want to do it and we will not want to do it again," Tsang said. \par \par \uc2  - Hong Kong Newsroom (852) 2843 6933; Fax (852) 2845 0636 \par \par \uc2  - Email: hongkong.newsroom@reuters.com. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021217du9700wiq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
