\par\par \b \uc2  HK lawmakers angry at government over  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tan Ee Lyn \par \uc2 511 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 September 1998\par 10:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 HONG KONG\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Sept 7 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  legislators lashed out at the government on Monday for a lack of transparency and the billions of dollars spent in recent weeks in defence of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's linked exchange rate. \par \par \uc2  At a two-hour meeting with Financial Secretary Donald Tsang, lawmakers kept up a vitriolic campaign, pressing him to disclose the amount spent and names of speculators whom the government has said were trying to drive down the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar. \par \par \uc2  The confrontation came after the government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in stock and futures markets last month to foil speculators, who it said had attacked the Hong Kong dollar to drive interest rates up and stock prices down to take profits on short stock positions. Experts have estimated the government has bought more than HK$100 billion (US$12.9 billion) worth of blue chip stocks to prop up the Hang Seng index with the aim of squeezing many short sellers into covering their positions at higher costs. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and particularly the amount spent, has become highly politicised during the current economic downturn and many legislators used Monday's forum to unleash their anger. \par \par \uc2  "You have not consulted us, you have spent billions of dollars and you won't even tell us how much you spent!" said feisty legislator Emily Lau, who heads the The Frontier party. \par \par \uc2  "Tell us, what if the speculators don't leave? Why on earth have we spent all that money? Have we achieved our aim? Do you think you are accounting for your actions by just saying a few words to reporters? I think it is just ludicrous," she said. \par \par \uc2  While the confrontation was peppered with fiery accusations and sarcasm, Tsang, in a grey suit and trademark bow-tie, kept an inscrutable expression. \par \par \uc2  "I thank legislator Lau for her lecture," Tsang said, drawing uproarious laughter from the chamber. \par \par \uc2  "But I hope legislators will be patient. When speculators leave, I will give a fuller account. If we disclose the amount now, it'll only benefit others, not ourselves," Tsang said. \par \par \uc2  Li Wah-ming of the popular Democratic Party pressed: "Who are these people (speculators), I'd love to know. Is it (George) Soros? Which hedge fund? We should come out in the open." \par \par \uc2  Tsang said: "If you want definite evidence, we won't know." He said deep-pocketed and organised speculators had been trying to attack the Hong Kong dollar in different ways, at many centres and under various fronts. \par \par \uc2  Cyd Ho of The Frontier party questioned if the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  might open the way for a conflict of interest for government officials, Kong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  (HKMA) employees and other people whom the government might have consulted. \par \par \uc2  But Tsang said there were clear rules regarding business interests and matters of conflict of interest governing all civil servants and HKMA colleagues. \par \par \uc2  Only a few legislators were able to put questions during the meeting. The meeting is to continue on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  (US$1 = HK$7.75). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021217du9700vr0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
