\par\par \b \uc2  HK govt acts to stabilise markets, hurts reputation. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Jennifer Genevieve \par \uc2 665 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 September 1998\par 12:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, Sept 7 (Reuters) - Hong Kong's reputation as a free market is set to take a hit by government moves to tighten securities regulations, but the stability provided by the measures is welcome after recent turbulence, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "Markets should probably be left to operate on a free basis. However the problem with Hong Kong is that it is open to manipulation and in this environment speculators can have a real effect on the real economy," said Jolyon Petch, head of Hong Kong research at ING Barings. \par \par \uc2  "It is probably negative but in this current environment, with crisis going on all around the world, the provision of stability is probably much more important than worrying about the perception of Hong Kong as a market," he said. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong government announced plans on Monday to beef up regulations in the stock and futures markets and possibly strengthen the Chief Executive's authority over those exchanges, in a bid to reduce opportunities to manipulate those markets. \par \par \uc2  The move came after the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in the stock and futures market last month to squeeze out speculators who had attacked the Hong Kong dollar and pushed interest rates higher while holding short positions in stocks and futures to benefit from falling share prices. \par \par \uc2  The government proposed strict rules on short-selling, including plans to criminalise unreported short-selling. It called for more transparency in the futures market and changes to Hong Kong's clearing rules to ensure timely settlement. \par \par \uc2  "They are going to be fiddly and picky. That is going to do nothing to enhance our reputation," said Steven Thompson, chief analyst at Nikko Research Center. \par \par \uc2  "It is quite clear that the stock exchange should be a separate entity to the government." \par \par \uc2  But some analysts said there were rule enforcement problems in Hong Kong. Howard Gorges, a director at South China Brokerage, said regulations had been particularly lax in the areas of short-selling and futures trading. \par \par \uc2  "It was too free. There were too many loopholes. This is not a big enough market to be totally free," he said. \par \par \uc2  "If people like to short sell, then they are not going to like it. But long-term portfolio investors will probably welcome measures which increase stability and reduce manipulation." \par \par \uc2  Analysts said government comments about increasing the Chief Executive's authority in financial markets were vague. "It might be necessary to strengthen the power of the Chief Executive to give direction to the exchanges and clearing houses to ensure that the government can react quickly whenever public interests are under threat," the government said in a statement. \par \par \uc2  "In times of emergency I guess you can do whatever it takes," said Frederick Tsang, a director at DBS Securities. \par \par \uc2  But he said it was important for the government to leave securities bodies to operate independently. "It would be OK if the government retreated from the market and then asked the stock exchange bodies to review the rules and regulations." \par \par \uc2  "The impression is not so good. Things are getting complicated," Tsang added. \par \par \uc2  The Hang Seng Index soared 588.29 points, or 7.86 percent, to close at 8,076.76 on Monday, boosted mainly by measures announced by the HKMA over the weekend to improve liquidity in the banking system and make Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system less susceptible to manipulation. \par \par \uc2  "There is improved liquidity in the system and improved transparency," said Andrew Look, regional director at Prudential Portfolio Managers (Asia). \par \par \uc2  "But one flaw, if you have to pick on the government, is that they should have done this before they \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market because if you have proper rules and regulations you probably do not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the first place and ruin the good reputation of \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  being a non-\b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  government." \par \par \uc2  ((\b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  Newsroom (852) 2843 6345; Fax (852) 2845 0636)) \par \par \uc2  ((Email: hongkong.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021217du9700vnv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
