\par\par \b \uc2  HK to detail shareholdings when market stabilises. \b0\par\par\uc2 489 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 September 1998\par 06:40\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, Sept 7 (Reuters) - Hong Kong Financial Secretary Donald Tsang said on Monday the government would give a full account of its shareholdings accumulated during recent stock market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  when speculators had left the market. \par \par \uc2  The government would set up a company under the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  which would manage the Hong Kong shareholdings and avoid situations of conflict of interest, Tsang told the Financial Affairs panel of the Legislative Council. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  government began \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the stock market on August 14 to chase out speculators who had engaged in a double play of attacking the Hong Kong dollar and pushing interest rates higher while holding short positions in the stock market to benefit from falling share prices. \par \par \uc2  Tsang said the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  had not been in the market since August 28. \par \par \uc2  "The stocks that we purchased during this period of time are very good investments. We will retreat from the market in an orderly manner after those speculators have left the market," Tsang said. \par \par \uc2  "We will fully report the stocks we hold to the Hong Kong people and we are also prepared to establish a company under the HKMA to manage these investments and try to avoid conflicts of interest." \par \par \uc2  Tsang said the professional management company would be created under the Exchange Fund as soon as possible. \par \par \uc2  Civil servants and HKMA employees were governed by strict rules, he told the Legislative Council meeting. \par \par \uc2  "There are clear rules regarding business interests and matters of conflict of interest governing all civil servants and HKMA colleagues." \par \par \uc2  Tsang said he was personally taking responsbility for the government's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "We feel that if we did not go into the market, the result would have been unimaginable." \par \par \uc2  "According to professional estimates, in view of the current low turnover in the stock market, plus the pressure on Hang Seng Index futures, if the situation had continued the Hang Seng Index would have dropped 2,000 to 3,000 points in a very short period of time." \par \par \uc2  Tsang declined to disclose the amount the government had spent in the stock and futures markets. \par \par \uc2  "I hope legislators will be patient. When speculators leave I will give a fuller account... If we disclose the amount now it will only help others, not ourselves," he said. \par \par \uc2  The government would not support the market but could not rule out the need for future \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he said. \par \par \uc2  But Tsang added that he hoped measures detailed over the weekend to strengthen the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board, as well as proposals announced on Monday to prevent manipulation in the stock and futures markets, would mean that future \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be avoided. \par \par \uc2  "We hope that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  government will not need to enter this kind of commercial market again," said Tsang. \par \par \uc2  ((Hong Kong Newsroom (852) 2843 6345; Fax (852) 2845 0636)) \par \par \uc2  ((Email: hongkong.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021217du9700ujg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pich