\par\par \b \uc2  Asia Crisis At A Glance: Dlr Drops To 4-Month Low Vs Yen \b0\par\par\uc2 447 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 September 1998\par 11:01\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  -- The dollar dropped to a four-month low against the yen on selling by overseas players over aggressive yen-supportive remarks by Japanese finance ministry officials; at 0815 GMT, the dollar was at Y131.97, down from Y133.82 late Friday in New York. \par \par \uc2  A correction to the excessive weakness of the yen and the excessive strength of the dollar is now beginning, said Japan Finance Ministry's vice minister of international affairs Sakakibara. \par \par \uc2  -- The Nikkei stock average surged almost 750 points, or 5.3%, to end at 14,790.06. Hedge funds and public funds actively bought futures as the yen's gain, the possibility of a U.S. interest rate cut and rises in Asian stock markets buoyed Tokyo shares. \par \par \uc2  -- Malaysian shares were up 22% late as local retail speculators and institutional investors, including a government investment arm and the Employees' Provident Fund, aggressively bought local shares. \par \par \uc2  -- Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Singson denied that the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  is considering implementing capital controls. \par \par \uc2  -- IMF Asia Pacific Director Neiss said now isn't the right time for Indonesia to apply a fixed exchange rate system, a local newspaper reported. \par \par \uc2  -- Hong Kong's Financial Secretary Tsang reiterated the government's pledge to maintain the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's link to the U.S. dollar. The Hong Kong government called for tighter discipline from regulators and outlined a 30-point plan to restore order to Hong Kong's markets. \par \par \uc2  -- Hong Kong shares ended 7.8% higher, lifted by the government's new measures to defend the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peg to the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  -- \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up share prices has damaged the territory's reputation as a free-market financial center, a Hong Kong legislator said. \par \par \uc2  -- The Philippine government and the IMF agreed on a 4.1% 1999 GNP growth rate target, Singson said. Over the weekend, the government and IMF agreed on a 1.5% GNP growth target for 1998. \par \par \uc2  -- A senior official of Japan's ruling Liberal Democratic Party, Fukaya, said he still hopes for a compromise with opposition parties on legislation to clean up the debt-ridden banking system. \par \par \uc2  -- Japan's chief government spokesman, Nonaka, said the government wasn't considering boosting the amount of public funds, now Y13 trillion, set aside to strengthen banks' capital basis. \par \par \uc2  -- An uplift of sentiment on regional stock exchanges and a rise in the yen pushed Singapore's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and stocks higher. In late trade, Singapore shares were up 6.9%, following a week of heavy losses in the wake of Malaysian uncertainty. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 07-09-98 \par \par \uc2  0901GMT \par \par \uc2  1042 4190 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du9707nj7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
