\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS - Hong Kong beefs up  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  board system. \b0\par\par\uc2 475 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 September 1998\par 08:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, Sept 5 (Reuters) - The Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  (HKMA) on Saturday announced measures intended to strengthen the territory's linked exchange rate system and reduce interest rate volatility. \par \par \uc2  The measures, which officials described as a "purification," of Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system, included specific rules for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  conversion and moves meant to bolster liquidity. \par \par \uc2  HKMA chief executive Joseph Yam told a news conference that the measures would make Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board arrangements less susceptible to manipulation by speculators. \par \par \uc2  "These measures aim at strengthening Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board arrangements and achieving an even higher degree of transparency and disclosure," Yam said. \par \par \uc2  "They will enhance the robustness of Hong Kong's monetary arangement, characterised by the linked exchange rate system." he added. "They should also help to reduce excessive volatility in interest rates." \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong has pegged its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the U.S. dollar at a rate of US$7.8 to per U.S. dollar since 1983. Last month, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  government began a market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  aimed at foiling speculators it accused of pressuring the local dollar to push up interest rates, drive down stock prices and profit from short positions in stocks and futures. \par \par \uc2  In one of the most important measures, the HKMA said it would provide a clear undertaking to all licensed banks in Hong Kong to convert Hong Kong dollars in their clearing accounts into U.S. dollars at the fixed exchange rate of HK$7.75 per U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  The move makes explicit the HKMA's persistent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the money markets at that rate and was meant to clearly demonstrate the authority's commitment to the peg system. \par \par \uc2  The HKMA added that the government remained fully commited to the linked exchange rate system and "strongly reiterates that there will be no \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls" in Hong Kong. \par \par \uc2  In addition to the seven measures, the HKMA said it would work toward producing an Exchange Fund balance sheet showing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  and have this published as frequently as is technically feasible. \par \par \uc2  The HKMA said its intention was to move the convertibility rate to HK$7.80 when market circumstances permit. Adopting that rate now would cause market volatility because the local dollar has been trading around HK$7.75 for some time. \par \par \uc2  The authority also replaced its Liquidity Adjustment Facility (LAF) with a discount window with a base rate to be determined from time to time by the HKMA. \par \par \uc2  Major Hong Kong banks reacted positively to the announcements, saying they would reduce financial market volatility. \par \par \uc2  "These are sensible measure which strengthen the \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Board arrangements in Hong Kong and should have a stabilising effect on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and money markets, said Mervyn Davies, a group executive director for Standard Chartered Bank. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021217du9500tzb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*