\par\par \b \uc2  HKMA to hold news conference at 0315 GMT. \b0\par\par\uc2 215 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 September 1998\par 04:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, Sept 5 (Reuters) - The Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced it would hold a news conference chaired by its chief executive Joseph Yam at 11:15 a.m (0315 GMT) on Saturday, following a meeting with banks. \par \par \uc2  "The press conference will address the strengthening of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board arrangements in Hong Kong," an HKMA spokeswoman said. "The press conference will take place after the HKMA meets with banks." \par \par \uc2  The HKMA has invited banks to a Saturday morning meeting in Hong Kong to discuss "recent market developments," an HKMA spokesman in New York said on Friday. \par \par \uc2  He ruled out the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls. The Hong Kong government is estimated to have spent about HK$120 billion (US$15 billion) last month as it boosted share prices to squeeze out short sellers. \par \par \uc2  The government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which officials have argued was aimed at protecting the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar, sent shock waves around the world. \par \par \uc2  The Financial Affairs panel of the Legislative Council is due to meet on Monday to question the government about recent massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the stock and futures markets. The government has said it will use the occasion to detail how it plans to make those markets less vulnerable to manipulation. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021217du9500tt5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
