\par\par \b \uc2  Moody's Says It May Lower Debt Ratings For China, Hong Kong \b0\par\par\uc2 1,118 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 September 1998\par 15:12\par \uc2  Dow Jones Online News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Moody's Investors Service Inc. Friday said it is reviewing for a possible downgrade its credit ratings on the foreign debt of China and Hong Kong, citing doubts about recent efforts to prop up the value of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and economic weakness in general. \par \par \uc2  Moody's said China's single-A-1 long-term debt rating and the Baa-2 rating for the country's four state-owned banks are at risk of a downgrade. Single-A-1 in Moody's system is middle investment grade, while Baa-2 is two notches above "junk" status. \par \par \uc2  "Today's action was prompted by mounting pressures on China's external position from export weakness and declining foreign direct investment, both of which are important sources of economic growth in China," Moody's said. \par \par \uc2  Moody's said it will consider how much support the banks enjoy from the government. Earlier this week, Moody's cut ratings on China's big state-owned investment companies because it sees less support from the government. \par \par \uc2  The agency is also looking at Hong Kong's long-term single-A-1 rating, citing Hong Kong's status as part of China and trade ties with the mainland. In particular, Moody's is concerned about whether the Chinese yuan will be devalued and confidence in the Hong Kong dollar's link to the U.S. dollar. However, Moody's offered no prediction about whether the yuan will be devalued. \par \par \uc2  Moody's noted China's "sizable official \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves and current account surplus" but spoke of a "continued deep slump in regional economic and trade performance as well as in a retrenchment of global capital market funds for emerging markets in general." Moody's said it is uncertain whether China's effort to sustain economic growth and minimize unemployment will "complicate" the country's "enormous task" of reforming its state-owned enterprises. \par \par \uc2  China's usually quiet \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has made loud pledges in recent weeks that it won't devalue the yuan anytime soon. But rumors continue to make the rounds in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and stock markets around the world that China will do so. Many people fear that a devalued yuan would further batter Asia's depreciated \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , spreading the region's economic dislocation further West. Experts say the pledge appears to have teeth but investors and traders were burned many times in the past year as one Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after another collapsed despite assurances of stability. \par \par \uc2  The devaluation rumors have stepped up in recent weeks because of a slowing Chinese economy and weak yen, accompanied by black-market prices for the yuan plunging to depths not seen since Beijing last devalued the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in early 1994. China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been adjusting interest rates to prop up the yuan and the Hong Kong dollar, the only two major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asia to have so far escaped devaluation in the region's economic crisis. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong has been hurt by the region's financial-markets turbulence and by speculation in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Moody's said. Confidence in the Hong Kong dollar's "peg" is also being hurt by uncertainty over the yuan. "Together, these factors are causing the most serious economic downturn in Hong Kong's recent history, bringing about a deterioration in Hong Kong's traditionally strong government financial position," Moody's said. \par \par \uc2  Moody's also expects to see a "significant drop" in Hong Kong's reserves after the territory spent billions in recent weeks to prop up the value of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and stock markets. "\b \highlight22\uc2 Interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the future could weaken the foreign asset position further," Moody's said. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Friday that it will conduct a series of briefings Saturday to announce a reinforcement of the Hong Kong dollar's peg to the U.S. dollar. "This is to announce various measures we have derived to strengthen the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system," a spokesman for the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  said. He added that there was "absolutely" no thought of Hong Kong abandoning the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system, widening the Hong Kong dollar's trading range or changing its parity against the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  Moody's will also review the ratings of several Hong Kong financial institutions for possible downgrade. "Pressures on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  are expected to keep interest rates high, putting pressures on interest margins and eventually on borrowers," Moody's said. \par \par \uc2  Lost in much of the talk about a possible yuan devaluation is the potential impact on Hong Kong, a major trade center in Asia that was returned to Chinese rule last year. Some experts think a yuan devaluation would mean the end of Hong Kong as a financial center. \par \par \uc2  Many analysts stress that fundamentals in China - however weak - haven't changed all that much in recent weeks. They also point to the fact that China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is convertible only for trade in goods and services, or what's called the current account. It can't be exchanged under the capital, or investment, account, meaning speculators can't trade it solely for profit. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  threw another wrench into the works. For two weeks, the Hong Kong government waged a battle against speculators who have tried to force a devaluation of the Hong Kong dollar. The government abruptly withdrawed from the effort this week, triggering a sharp drop in Hong Kong's stock market. \par \par \uc2  The deteriorating state of the global economy made Hong Kong's campaign to support its markets extremely costly. Traders estimate the government spent between $12 billion and $15 billion on shares in the effort - more than half of that in an extraordinary buying spree last Friday. Even with its vast \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  fund - worth roughly $97 billion - most analysts said Hong Kong can't afford such a battle for more than a few weeks. \par \par \uc2  Experts are now focusing on how Hong Kong officials plan to extricate themselves from the standoff. The government spent this week penning in its foes with new regulations. With the rule changes, it is now more expensive to attack the market by short-selling and more difficult to accumulate big positions secretly. The new rules, while not onerous by the standards of U.S. and U.K. markets, are a big change for Hong Kong, long considered one of least-regulated large markets. \par \par \uc2  In fact, the past few weeks were so strange that investors largely ignored bad news from credit-rating agencies. On Monday, Standard & Poor's downgraded the territory's foreign debt rating and its short-term local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rating. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1998 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020329du94017ho\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict