\par\par \b \uc2  Asia Crisis/Glance: China, HK Rtgs On Review, Dn - Moody's \b0\par\par\uc2 435 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 September 1998\par 11:04\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  -- Moody's signaled that China's A1 foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt rating is at risk for a downgrade, due to deteriorating external positions and said it'll review Hong Kong's A3 long-term and Prime-2 short-term foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ceilings for possible downgrade,  and the ratings of several Hong Kong financial institutions for possible downgrade. \par \par \uc2  -- Malaysia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will change the rate of the ringgit's fixing to the dollar when structural changes in market demand and supply conditions necessitate it, a Bank Negara spokeswoman said. \par \par \uc2  -- Fitch IBCA downgraded Bank Bumiputra Malaysia and Sime Bank because of their recapitalization needs. \par \par \uc2  -- Ousted Malaysian Finance Minister Anwar said he may form a movement to push for political reform, and compared corruption in Malaysia to that of Suharto's Indonesia. He warned that if he's arrested under the Internal Security Act, hundreds will follow him.  The Muslim Youth Movement of Malaysia condemned the sacking of Anwar as a political conspiracy. \par \par \uc2  -- Recent economic indicators in Japan underline the fact that it has no other option but to keep its low interest rate policy, said Shinozuka, a member of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy-making board. \par \par \uc2  -- The dollar surged about Y1, as short-covering of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was accelerated by Shinozuka's pessimistic remarks on Japan's economy and on dollar-supportive comments by the Bundesbank's Tietmeyer. At 0800 GMT, the U.S. dollar was at Y135.72, up from Y134.24 late Thursday in New York. \par \par \uc2  -- Director-General Sakaiya of Japan's Economic Planning Agency said the state of the economy is becoming more severe, but "slumping" will still describe it in September's economic report. \par \par \uc2  -- Japanese companies are thought to have suffered valuation losses of Y2.9 trillion on their stock holdings, fanning fears that the recent stock market decline would have a huge debilitating impact on corporate profitability for this fiscal year ending March 31, a research arm of Daiwa Securities Co. said. \par \par \uc2  -- Asian stock markets were mixed Friday. The Nikkei ended 1.5% lower following overnight losses on Wall Street and as sentiment weakened following the failure of Toa Steel, Japan's biggest manufacturing failure since World War II. local retailers searched for bargains. Indonesia's main stock index was 3.2% lower.  Taiwan shares ended 3.4% higher. Philippine shares ended down 2.8% \par \par \uc2  -- Two leading \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  opposition leaders said the government's recent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the territory's financial markets was damaging foreign investor confidence. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 04-09-98 \par \par \uc2  0904GMT \par \par \uc2  1042 4190 55126 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du9407lsd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
