\par\par \b \uc2  ASIA MARKETS - Wall St helps, Malaysia confounds. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,129 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 September 1998\par 12:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2      SINGAPORE, Sept 2 (Reuters) - Wall Street's strong \par \par \uc2  overnight rebound and Malaysia's moves to effectively kill \par \par \uc2  offshore ringgit trading dominated activity in Asian markets on \par \par \uc2  Wednesday. \par \par \uc2      The dollar was off its earlier highs against the yen and \par \par \uc2  mark on profit-taking and dealers said it was likely to make \par \par \uc2  slow progress as markets worldwide scrutinised Wall Street for \par \par \uc2  cues, while keeping an eye on Russia's woes. \par \par \uc2      MALAYSIA MOVES CONFOUND MARKETS \par \par \uc2      Utter confusion over the implication of Malaysia's newly \par \par \uc2  announced \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and capital controls characterised trading \par \par \uc2  in the ringgit and Kuala Lumpur stock market. \par \par \uc2      Dealers said offshore trading in spot and forward ringgit \par \par \uc2  had ground to a halt as banks tried to sort out how to settle \par \par \uc2  previous transactions. \par \par \uc2      Bank Negara's statement that all transactions executed \par \par \uc2  before September would have to be settled by September 4 did \par \par \uc2  not address the problem of forward contracts. \par \par \uc2      The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  later said it was extending the deadline \par \par \uc2  to September 9. \par \par \uc2      Bank Negara also fixed the exchange rate at 3.80 to the \par \par \uc2  dollar, effective immediately, for an indefinite period. \par \par \uc2      Dealers said any actual trade in the ringgit on Wednesday \par \par \uc2  was commercially driven and confined to banks in Malaysia. \par \par \uc2      Offshore trading of the ringgit dried up, especially in \par \par \uc2  Singapore, its main centre, and there was talk that banks were \par \par \uc2  meeting local authorities to work out a settlement procedure. \par \par \uc2      While Malaysia's actions do seem likely to guarantee a \par \par \uc2  stable ringgit, analysts and fund managers said foreign \par \par \uc2  investors were taking a dim view of what amounted to a retreat \par \par \uc2  from the world economy. \par \par \uc2  "What's happening in Malaysia actually scares investors \par \par \uc2  off. It shows governments can turn around and change their \par \par \uc2  policies very quickly and there are no reassurances for \par \par \uc2  investors," said Slavia Cheong, investment manager at Aberdeen \par \par \uc2  Asset Management in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  The KLSE stock index staged a sharp rebound, bouncing over \par \par \uc2  12 percent, from Tuesday's 13 percent plunge as investors \par \par \uc2  scrambled to cover short stock positions amid confusion over \par \par \uc2  the new trading curbs. \par \par \uc2  The stock exchange clarified that the new rules did not \par \par \uc2  impose a one-year moratorium on the sale of shares, but \par \par \uc2  proceeds from such sales would have to be kept in Malaysia for \par \par \uc2  at least one year. \par \par \uc2  REST OF ASIA RESISTS MALAYSIA'S EXAMPLE \par \par \uc2  Other Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose as investors closed \par \par \uc2  out short positions amid worries that more governments in the \par \par \uc2  region might go the same way as Malaysia. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar, which along with the ringgit had been \par \par \uc2  among the most liquid of the regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was off its \par \par \uc2  highs in late trade after jumping sharply on long U.S. dollar \par \par \uc2  liquidations by hedge funds. \par \par \uc2  But most governments appeared keen on distancing themselves \par \par \uc2  from Malaysia's decision, spurring some short-covering of the \par \par \uc2  U.S. dollar in late trade. \par \par \uc2  Indonesia's top economics minister Ginandjar Kartasasmita \par \par \uc2  said his country would stick to a free \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  system \par \par \uc2  while continuing to monitor developments in Malaysia. \par \par \uc2  Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Chatu Mongol Sonakul said it was \par \par \uc2  not planning to follow Malaysia's example as the situation in \par \par \uc2  Thailand had stabilised and introducing new controls would do \par \par \uc2  more harm than good. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Thailand's outstanding foreign debt \par \par \uc2  at the end of June was $88.1 billion and about 68 percent of \par \par \uc2  total debt is long-term. \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG STOCKS GAIN, INTERBANK RATES DROP \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong's blue chip index briefly climbed above the key \par \par \uc2  7,500 barrier on short-covering fuelled by Wall Street's \par \par \uc2  rebound, a firmer yen and lower interbank rates. \par \par \uc2  But brokers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent \par \par \uc2  aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to squeeze speculators out of stock \par \par \uc2  and futures markets had sparked some concern about the \par \par \uc2  territory's free market mechanism, especially in the light of \par \par \uc2  Malaysia's moves. \par \par \uc2  HKMA deputy chief executive David Carse said overnight in \par \par \uc2  London the authority had not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in stock and futures \par \par \uc2  markets on Monday and Tuesday, but was willing to do so again \par \par \uc2  if necessary. \par \par \uc2  PHILIPPINES PESO UNIMPRESSED BY REMOVAL OF LIMIT \par \par \uc2  The peso was unable to sustain early gains despite the \par \par \uc2  Philippine Monetary Board's removal of a limit that allowed \par \par \uc2  banks to sell dollars worth up to 20 percent of their capital. \par \par \uc2  The Board retained a five percent limit on overbought \par \par \uc2  dollar positions, and a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said the removal \par \par \uc2  of the oversold ceiling would give an added boost to the peso's \par \par \uc2  recent uptrend. \par \par \uc2      But bankers said the ruling was academic since the dollar \par \par \uc2  remained in favour amid regional volatility. \par \par \uc2      KOREAN ECONOMY SEEN RECOVERING NEXT YEAR \par \par \uc2      In South Korea, the finance ministry repeated a recent GDP \par \par \uc2  forecast of minus five percent for 1998 against last year's 5.5 \par \par \uc2  percent rise, but said the economy was likely to grow by two \par \par \uc2  percent next year. \par \par \uc2  It said a current account surplus of $37 billion was \par \par \uc2  expected this year compared with an $8.2 billion dollar deficit \par \par \uc2  in 1997. \par \par \uc2  The finance ministry said it would continue to lower \par \par \uc2  interest rates through flexible monetary and fiscal policies. \par \par \uc2       Change on the day WED 02 1011GMT \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ...Latest.....Prev Close...Pct Move \par \par \uc2   Ringgit....3.80.........3.93.........3.42% \par \par \uc2   Rupiah.....10800.00.....10700.00....-0.93% \par \par \uc2   Baht.......40.70........40.80........0.25% \par \par \uc2   Peso.......43.20........43.00.......-0.46% \par \par \uc2   Sing Dlr...1.74.........1.73........-0.60% \par \par \uc2   Taiwan Dlr.34.77........34.81........0.12% \par \par \uc2   Korean Won.1339.00......1354.00......1.12% \par \par \uc2   Change since start of Asia crisis \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ...Latest.....July '97