\par\par \b \uc2  HK gov't blasts S&P cut, analysts take other view. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Andrea Ricci \par \uc2 751 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 September 1998\par 05:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, Sept 1 (Reuters) - The Hong Kong government on Tuesday called unfair the territory's downgrade by Standard & Poor's Corp but analysts said the cut was hardly surprising. \par \par \uc2  "The government does not find S&P's decision to downgrade the credit ratings of Hong Kong justified," the government said. \par \par \uc2  S&P cut the long-and short-term foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  sovereign ratings of Hong Kong to Single-A/A-1 from A-plus/A-1-plus. \par \par \uc2  It also cut Hong Kong's long-and short-term local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ratings to A-plus/A-1 from AA-minus/A-1-plus. \par \par \uc2  S&P said the downgrades reflected a decline in Hong Kong's financial flexibility due to the growing domestic impact of East Asia's recession, as well as the large official purchases in the local equity and futures markets. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  (HKMA) has been aggressively buying shares to foil speculators that had shorted the stock market and then attacked the Hong Kong dollar with the aim of pushing interest rates up and stock prices down. \par \par \uc2  In its objection to S&P's action, the government said its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was meant to deter manipulation, not support stocks. \par \par \uc2  But S&P managing director for Asia Paul Coughlin said it was not the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  itself that was causing the rating agency concern, but rather what it said about the state of Hong Kong's economy and what it might mean for its future stability. \par \par \uc2  "We are not necessarily saying the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was the wrong thing to do. We are saying that the margin of safety has been eroded, increasing Hong Kong's risk," said Coughlin. \par \par \uc2  S&P noted in its ratings statement that by some estimates the government had spent up to US$15 billion buying shares and futures "as part of an increasingly complex and risky strategy to deter speculative pressures." \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was likely to have some effect on foreign reserves, although the exact impact was not yet clear. \par \par \uc2  And even if the government stopped \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the HKMA's portfolio was likely to suffer significant losses unless the global trend of falling equity prices was reversed, S&P said. \par \par \uc2  HKMA assets backing the local dollar would fall in any case as funds were diverted to the treasury to finance a fiscal gap. \par \par \uc2  Bear Stearns sovereign analyst John Seel said the worry with the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not the money spent, but what it said about Hong Kong. \par \par \uc2  "The important thing...is the fact that Hong Kong is becoming less transparent and the market mechanism is not being able to act as freely as it had in the past," he said. \par \par \uc2  Even more troublesome for Hong Kong were the stresses affecting the financial sector, S&P said. \par \par \uc2  Lehman Brothers economist Rob Subbaraman said S&P's concerns were valid. "In an economy contracting five percent there will be corporate failures and it will affect the banking sector, though how severely is an open question," he said. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong government on Friday estimated economic activity had fallen by about five percent in the second quarter of 1998 and forecast a full-year fall of around four percent. \par \par \uc2  Even after the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the HKMA would have ample reserves to back the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board, but problems could arise if the financial system were to weaken seriously, Subbaraman said. \par \par \uc2  "The risk is that if some banks..did develop problems and there were runs on banks, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board could fall apart because the Exchange Fund does not have enough money to cover all deposits," said Subbaraman. \par \par \uc2  In July, before the stock market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 's foreign reserves were US$96.5 billion, eight times the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in circulation and about 44 percent of M3, the broadest measure of money supply. \par \par \uc2  Bonds of Hong Kong entities showed little reaction to the S&P news, but analysts noted that the bonds had been trading more like BB than Single-A credits for some time. \par \par \uc2  Spreads widened just slightly, said Chris Francis, head of credit research at Merrill Lynch in Hong Kong. \par \par \uc2  "In the past few weeks, Asian spreads have been much more affected by external events, such as Russia, than anything going on in the region," he said. \par \par \uc2  - Hong Kong Newsroom (852) 2843-6371; Fax (852) 2845-0636 \par \par \uc2  - Email: hongkong.newsroom@reuters.com. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du9201zz4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
