\par\par \b \uc2  Economist Friedman Says  \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong \highlight\uc2  Erred Badly In Market  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2  By Erik Guyot, Staff Reporter \par \uc2 422 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 September 1998\par 00:33\par \uc2  Dow Jones Online News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 HONG KONG\b0 \highlight\uc2  -(Dow Jones)- Nobel Laureate Milton Friedman Wednesday declared that "all bets are off" as to whether Hong Kong can keep its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pegged to the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  Friedman, one of the best-known champions of Hong Kong's free-market system, also blasted the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  government's recent stock market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as "insane." \par \par \uc2  In a telephone interview from his home in San Francisco, he said that "if the Hong Kong government wants to restore its system, it should announce that it made a mistake and immediately sell off whatever stocks it purchased." \par \par \uc2  Friedman's criticism adds to a growing chorus of concern over the Hong Kong government's decision to buy stocks as an indirect means of deterring \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators. \par \par \uc2  The 1976 winner of the Nobel Prize in economics for his contributions to monetary theory, Friedman is famed for his statement that to see "how the free market really works, Hong Kong is the place to go." \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  - the city's de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  - has spent an estimated US$15 billion to deter \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators from what it calls unfair attacks on the Hong Kong dollar. \par \par \uc2  Under Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -board system, when speculators sell the Hong Kong dollar, this automatically pushes up local interest rates. Those higher interest rates eventually attract some investors to buy Hong Kong dollars, bolstering the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But steeper borrowing costs also slam the economy and share prices. \par \par \uc2  Speculators, say government officials, have been reaping huge profits by short-selling stocks - borrowing stock and selling it on the expectation that the price would fall - and then replacing the borrowed stock at a profit. The government's efforts to push up the stock market, say officials, have prevented speculators from making money and kept them at bay. \par \par \uc2  Friedman said the government's involvement in the stock market was totally unnecessary and counterproductive for the defense of Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The economist said that if the government let the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board function properly, the automatic rise in interest rates would be sufficient to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  impervious to all speculative assaults. \par \par \uc2  "If they continued to operate the system as it was set up in 1983, it would be as sound as the Rock of Gibraltar," Friedman said. \par \par \uc2  A spokesman from the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  declined to comment on Friedman's remarks. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1998 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020329du92016uu\par }\par \b \uc2 Zusammenfassung der Suche\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Text\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctl